const PAGES=[{"id": "home", "slug": "", "title": "Start here"}, {"id": "cell", "slug": "genome-genes-chromatin", "title": "Genome, genes & chromatin"}, {"id": "dogma", "slug": "central-dogma", "title": "The central dogma"}, {"id": "splicing", "slug": "splicing", "title": "Splicing & isoforms"}, {"id": "mutations", "slug": "mutations-genetic-variation", "title": "Mutations & variation"}, {"id": "omes", "slug": "types-of-omics", "title": "Map of the -omes"}, {"id": "generation", "slug": "how-omics-data-are-generated", "title": "How omics data are generated"}, {"id": "genomics", "slug": "genomics", "title": "Genomics"}, {"id": "epigenomics", "slug": "epigenomics", "title": "Epigenomics"}, {"id": "transcriptomics", "slug": "transcriptomics", "title": "Transcriptomics (bulk)"}, {"id": "singlecell", "slug": "single-cell-spatial-omics", "title": "Single-cell & spatial"}, {"id": "proteomics", "slug": "proteomics", "title": "Proteomics"}, {"id": "metabolomics", "slug": "metabolomics-lipidomics", "title": "Metabolomics"}, {"id": "lipidomics", "slug": "lipidomics-targeted", "title": "Lipidomics (targeted)"}, {"id": "microbiome", "slug": "metagenomics-microbiome", "title": "Metagenomics & microbiome"}, {"id": "more", "slug": "other-omics", "title": "More omics"}, {"id": "relations", "slug": "multi-omics-biology", "title": "How the layers talk"}, {"id": "integration", "slug": "multi-omics-integration", "title": "Integrating multi-omics data"}, {"id": "practice", "slug": "workflow-reproducibility", "title": "Workflow & reproducibility"}, {"id": "atlas", "slug": "bioinformatics-tool-atlas", "title": "Tool atlas"}, {"id": "benchmarks", "slug": "benchmarks", "title": "Benchmark hub"}, {"id": "gaps", "slug": "research-gaps", "title": "Research questions & gaps"}, {"id": "learn", "slug": "learning-path-glossary", "title": "Learning path & glossary"}, {"id": "contact", "slug": "contact", "title": "Contact"}];
const SEARCH=[{"id": "home", "title": "Start here", "text": "A field guide for bioinformaticians · from first principles to open problems Every layer of the cell, read as data. This guide walks from the chemistry of DNA to the algorithms that turn sequencers, mass spectrometers and microscopes into biological knowledge. For each omics layer it explains what is measured, why it matters, how the data are processed, which tools do each step, what independent benchmarks found, and which questions are still open. Written and maintained by Kianoosh Naghibzadeh Toronto Metropolitan University · The Hospital for Sick Children (SickKids) · Toronto, Canada Fig. 0 · A Sanger-style chromatogram of a coding sequence. The four trace colours are reused for the four molecular layers throughout the guide. The layers, top to bottom Information flows down this stack, from the relatively stable genome to the fast-changing metabolome. The bar shows roughly how dynamic each layer is (how fast it changes within a cell or person). Click a layer to open its chapter. Genome The DNA sequence: ~3.1 billion base pairs per haploid human genome, ~20,000 protein-coding genes. Mostly fixed for life, except somatic mutation. DNA · years–lifetime Epigenome Chemical marks and packaging that decide which DNA is readable: CpG methylation, histone modifications, accessibility, 3D folding. chromatin · days–years Transcriptome All RNA molecules: mRNAs and their isoforms, lncRNAs, miRNAs. A snapshot of which genes are being used right now. RNA · minutes–hours Proteome Proteins, proteoforms and their modifications, abundances, locations and interactions: the cell's working machinery. protein · hours–days Metabolome Small molecules (<1,500 Da): sugars, amino acids, lipids, drugs. The closest molecular readout of phenotype. metabolite · seconds–minutes Microbiome Genomes and functions of the microbes living with us; a second genome that shapes our metabolome and immunity. community · days–months Phenome Traits, disease and clinical outcomes. Multi-omics is ultimately about explaining how variation at the top produces variation here. organism · integrated How to read the tool tables Every chapter lists the main tools for that layer. Each tool carries two kinds of \"level\", because people mean both when they ask about a tool's level. Analysis stage Primary Raw instrument signal to usable reads or spectra: base calling, demultiplexing, signal processing. Secondary Reads or spectra to features: alignment, quantification, variant calling, peak calling, identification. Tertiary Features to biology: statistics, differential analysis, annotation, networks, integration, interpretation. Skill level ● ●● Entry: GUI, web server or one-command pipeline; sensible defaults. ●● ● Intermediate: scripting in R or Python, choosing parameters, understanding the model. ●●● Advanced: method-level understanding, HPC or GPU, custom models, many parameters that change the answer. Callouts Key idea The concept to remember. Benchmark What independent comparisons found. Research gap An open problem, often a thesis topic. Pitfall A common way to get it wrong. Suggested routes through the guide New to biology ≈ 3 weeks Genome, genes & chromatin Central dogma Splicing Mutations Map of the omes Learning path New to computation ≈ 6 weeks Learning path Workflow & reproducibility Bulk transcriptomics Genomics Single-cell & spatial Starting a PhD ongoing How the layers relate Multi-omics integration Benchmark hub Research questions & gaps Tool atlas About this guide Tool versions and benchmark rankings change quickly. Benchmarks are cited by first author, year and journal; each citation links to a PubMed search so you can read the original and check for newer work. When a statement is a judgement rather than a published result, the text says so."}, {"id": "cell", "title": "Genome, genes & chromatin", "text": "Foundations Genome, genes & chromatin Before any algorithm makes sense you need a clear picture of the molecules being measured: what DNA is, how a gene is laid out, and how two metres of DNA fit inside a nucleus while staying readable. DNA: the molecule DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) is a polymer of four nucleotides. Each nucleotide has a deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate and one of four bases: the purines adenine (A) and guanine (G) , and the pyrimidines cytosine (C) and thymine (T) . Two strands wind into a right-handed double helix and pair by hydrogen bonds: A with T (two bonds) and G with C (three bonds). Because G–C pairs are stronger, the GC content of a region affects its melting temperature, how well it is captured and amplified in library preparation, and therefore sequencing coverage. GC bias is a recurring source of technical artefacts in nearly every sequencing-based omics assay. The strands are antiparallel : one runs 5′→3′, the other 3′→5′. Polymerases only synthesise in the 5′→3′ direction, so by convention sequences are written 5′→3′. A gene can sit on either strand, which is why annotation files (GTF/GFF) carry a + or − strand field and why \"strand-specific\" RNA-seq libraries matter: they tell you which strand an RNA was transcribed from, so overlapping antisense genes can be separated. Key idea Base complementarity is the physical principle behind most of the field: hybridisation arrays, PCR, probe capture, bisulfite conversion readouts, CRISPR guide targeting and sequencing-by-synthesis all depend on A·T and G·C pairing. How the human genome is organised Feature Approximate size (human) Why it matters for analysis Haploid genome ~3.1 Gb (T2T-CHM13: 3.055 Gb, gap-free) Sets sequencing depth: 30× WGS ≈ 93 Gb of sequence per sample. Chromosomes 22 autosome pairs + XX or XY; mitochondrial DNA 16,569 bp in hundreds to thousands of copies per cell Sex chromosomes need ploidy-aware variant calling; mtDNA shows heteroplasmy (a fraction of copies mutated). Protein-coding genes ~19,000–20,000 (GENCODE) Coding sequence is only ~1–2% of the genome; exome sequencing targets this ~30–60 Mb. Non-coding RNA genes ~20,000+ lncRNA genes, plus miRNA, snRNA, snoRNA, tRNA, rRNA Many have poorly understood functions; annotation is still changing between releases. Repeats ~50%: transposable elements (LINEs ~17%, SINEs/Alu ~11%, LTRs, DNA transposons), satellites, segmental duplications (~5–7%) Short reads map ambiguously to repeats, producing low mapping quality and false variants. Long reads and T2T references help. Centromeres & telomeres Megabase α-satellite arrays; TTAGGG telomere repeats Missing from GRCh38 until the T2T consortium filled them in 2022. The two reference assemblies you will meet are GRCh37/hg19 (2009, still used by many clinical labs) and GRCh38/hg38 (2013, current standard), plus the complete T2T-CHM13 (2022) and the Human Pangenome Reference (a graph of many haplotypes, first release 2023). Coordinates differ between builds, so mixing them is one of the most common silent errors. Use liftOver or CrossMap to convert, and always record the build. Anatomy of a protein-coding gene Reading 5′→3′ along the coding strand, a typical eukaryotic gene contains: Promoter : the region around the transcription start site (TSS) where RNA polymerase II and general transcription factors assemble. Core promoter elements include the TATA box (~25–30 bp upstream, in a minority of genes), the initiator and downstream promoter element. About 60–70% of human promoters sit in CpG islands , regions of high CpG density that are usually unmethylated when the gene is active. Enhancers and silencers : distal regulatory elements, sometimes hundreds of kilobases away, bound by sequence-specific transcription factors. They contact promoters through 3D chromatin looping. Most disease-associated GWAS variants fall in enhancers rather than in coding sequence. 5′ untranslated region (5′UTR) : transcribed but not translated; contains elements controlling ribosome recruitment, including upstream open reading frames (uORFs). Exons and introns : exons are retained in mature mRNA; introns are removed by splicing. Human genes average ~9–10 exons; introns are on average much longer than exons. Coding sequence (CDS) : from the start codon (AUG) to a stop codon (UAA, UAG, UGA), read in triplets. 3′UTR and polyadenylation signal : the AAUAAA hexamer directs cleavage and poly(A) tail addition. 3′UTRs carry microRNA binding sites and RNA-binding protein motifs that control stability, localisation and translation. Alternative polyadenylation produces isoforms with longer or shorter 3′UTRs. enhancer promoter TSS intron 1 intron 2 5′UTR 3′UTR ATG stop AATAAA → poly(A) 5′ ────────── coding strand ──────────▶ 3′ dark boxes = CDS exons · light boxes = UTR Fig. 1.1 · A three-exon gene. Enhancers can sit upstream, downstream or inside introns of the gene they regulate, or of a different gene. Chromatin: packaging that controls access DNA wraps ~147 bp at a time around a nucleosome , an octamer of histones H2A, H2B, H3 and H4. Nucleosomes are separated by linker DNA (bound by H1), giving the \"beads on a string\" fibre with a ~200 bp periodicity. This periodicity is visible directly in ATAC-seq fragment-length distributions and in cell-free DNA fragment sizes (~167 bp peak), which is why both are used to infer nucleosome positioning. Chromatin exists in two broad states: Euchromatin Open, gene-rich, transcriptionally active. Marked by H3K4me3 at active promoters, H3K27ac at active enhancers and promoters, H3K36me3 across transcribed gene bodies. Accessible to transcription factors and to Tn5 transposase (ATAC-seq) and DNase I. Heterochromatin Compact and repressed. Constitutive (H3K9me3; repeats, centromeres) or facultative (H3K27me3 deposited by Polycomb; developmentally silenced genes that can be reactivated). Usually high CpG methylation outside islands. At larger scales the genome folds into loops (often anchored by CTCF and cohesin), topologically associating domains (TADs) of roughly 100 kb–1 Mb that preferentially self-interact, and A/B compartments that separate active from inactive chromatin. These are measured with chromosome conformation capture (Hi-C, Micro-C) and covered in the epigenomics chapter . Disrupting a TAD boundary can let an enhancer activate the wrong gene; this mechanism explains some limb malformations and cancers. Cells, tissues and why context matters Almost every cell in a person carries the same genome, yet a neuron and a hepatocyte use very different sets of genes. Cell identity is established by transcription factors and maintained by the epigenome. For analysis this has three consequences: Bulk measurements average over cell types. A change in a bulk tissue profile can mean that cells changed state, or only that the cell-type proportions changed. Deconvolution and single-cell methods exist to separate these. Regulatory effects are context-specific. A variant may affect expression only in one cell type, developmental stage or stimulus condition, which is a central difficulty when interpreting GWAS hits. Somatic variation exists. Cells acquire mutations as they divide. Cancer is the extreme case, but normal tissues are also mosaics of clones, which matters for low-frequency variant detection. Pitfall Gene identifiers are a constant source of silent data loss. HGNC symbols change (for example, SEPT7 → SEPTIN7 , after spreadsheets kept converting it to a date), Ensembl IDs carry version suffixes ( ENSG00000141510.18 ), and one gene maps to many transcripts and proteins. Map on stable IDs, strip versions consistently and keep the mapping table you used."}, {"id": "dogma", "title": "The central dogma", "text": "Foundations The central dogma, step by step Francis Crick's 1958 statement is about the direction of sequence information: it can pass from nucleic acid to nucleic acid and from nucleic acid to protein, but never from protein back to nucleic acid. Each transfer step is a control point, an assay, and a place where mutations act. DNA genome + epigenome pre-mRNA exons + introns mRNA cap · CDS · poly(A) Protein folding · PTMs Metabolite enzyme products transcription splicing translation catalysis replication reverse transcription (retroviruses, retrotransposons, telomerase) RNA replication (RNA viruses) feedback: transcription factors, chromatin modifiers, splicing factors are themselves proteins Genomics Epigenomics Nascent RNA-seq (PRO-seq, GRO-seq) Transcriptomics Epitranscriptomics Translatomics (Ribo-seq) Proteomics Metabolomics Lipidomics Fig. 2.1 · Solid arrows: the general transfers. Dashed: special transfers. Dotted amber: the feedback loop that makes the system a network, not a pipeline. Bottom row: the omics that measure each node. Replication Before a cell divides it copies its genome. DNA polymerases δ and ε synthesise new strands with an intrinsic error rate of roughly 10 −4 –10 −5 , which proofreading (3′→5′ exonuclease) and mismatch repair (MMR) lower to about 10 −9 –10 −10 per base per division. When proofreading (POLE, POLD1 mutations) or MMR (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) fails, tumours become hypermutated or microsatellite-unstable. These tumours carry characteristic mutational signatures and respond well to immune checkpoint inhibitors because their many mutations create neoantigens. That chain from a repair defect to a sequencing signature to a treatment decision is one of the clearest examples of omics in the clinic. Transcription RNA polymerase II reads the template strand and produces RNA that matches the coding strand (with U in place of T). Transcription has distinct, separately regulated phases: Initiation : transcription factors bind enhancers and promoters; the Mediator complex bridges them to the pre-initiation complex (TFIID with TATA-binding protein, TFIIB, TFIIE, TFIIF, TFIIH). TFIIH phosphorylates serine 5 of the Pol II C-terminal domain (CTD). Promoter-proximal pausing : in metazoans Pol II often pauses 20–60 nt downstream of the TSS. Release by P-TEFb (CDK9) is a major regulatory step, which is why nascent-transcription assays (PRO-seq, GRO-seq, TT-seq) can reveal regulation that steady-state RNA-seq misses. Elongation : CTD serine 2 phosphorylation recruits splicing and 3′-end factors. Splicing is largely co-transcriptional, so elongation speed influences which exons are included. Termination : cleavage at the poly(A) site, poly(A) addition, and release of Pol II. Three other polymerases have dedicated jobs: Pol I makes ribosomal RNA, Pol III makes tRNAs, 5S rRNA and some small RNAs, and mitochondrial RNA polymerase transcribes the mitochondrial genome. Ribosomal RNA is 80% or more of total RNA, which is why RNA-seq libraries either select poly(A)+ RNA or deplete rRNA. The choice changes what you can see: poly(A) selection loses histone mRNAs, many lncRNAs and most intronic reads, while rRNA depletion keeps them. RNA processing 5′ capping A 7-methylguanosine cap is added co-transcriptionally. It protects the RNA and recruits eIF4E for translation. Cap-based protocols (CAGE) map transcription start sites precisely. Splicing Introns are removed and exons joined by the spliceosome. More than 90–95% of multi-exon human genes are alternatively spliced. See the splicing chapter . 3′ end processing Cleavage and polyadenylation. About 70% of human genes have more than one poly(A) site (alternative polyadenylation, APA), changing 3′UTR length and therefore microRNA regulation. Editing and modification A-to-I editing by ADAR enzymes (read as A→G mismatches in RNA-seq), C-to-U editing by APOBEC1, and over 170 chemical modifications such as m6A, pseudouridine and m5C. This is the epitranscriptome. Export and decay Mature mRNAs are exported through nuclear pores. Decay pathways (deadenylation, decapping, exonucleases, nonsense-mediated decay) set mRNA half-lives from minutes to days. Non-coding RNA biogenesis microRNAs are processed by Drosha and Dicer into ~22-nt guides loaded into Argonaute; circular RNAs arise from back-splicing; lncRNAs are capped and often polyadenylated like mRNAs. Translation and the genetic code Ribosomes scan from the 5′ cap to the first suitable AUG (in a favourable Kozak context, gcc A cc AUG G ) and read the mRNA three nucleotides at a time. With 4 3 = 64 codons for 20 amino acids plus stop, the code is degenerate : most amino acids have several codons that usually differ at the third (\"wobble\") position. That is why many third-position changes are synonymous, and why the ratio of non-synonymous to synonymous substitutions (dN/dS) is used to detect selection and cancer driver genes. Translation has three phases: initiation (the most regulated, through eIF2 and eIF4F; the integrated stress response shuts it down by phosphorylating eIF2α), elongation (codon choice and tRNA availability affect speed and co-translational folding), and termination at a stop codon by eRF1/eRF3. Translation efficiency is measured by ribosome profiling (Ribo-seq), which sequences the ~30-nt mRNA fragments protected by ribosomes. After translation: the protein is not finished Polypeptides fold (often with chaperones), may be cleaved (signal peptides, pro-hormones), and receive post-translational modifications (PTMs) : phosphorylation (S, T, Y; signalling), acetylation (K; chromatin and metabolism), ubiquitination (K; degradation and signalling), glycosylation (N, S, T; secreted and membrane proteins), methylation, SUMOylation, lipidation and hundreds more. One gene can therefore give rise to many proteoforms , through alternative splicing, coding variants and PTM combinations. Protein half-lives range from minutes to months. Proteomics measures a cell state that is related to, but not determined by, the transcriptome. Why the dogma is a network The arrows look linear, but every step is controlled by products of later steps: transcription factors, splicing factors, RNA-binding proteins and chromatin modifiers are all proteins, and metabolites feed back too. Acetyl-CoA supplies histone acetylation, S-adenosylmethionine (SAM) supplies DNA and histone methylation, and α-ketoglutarate is a co-factor for TET demethylases. Mutations in the metabolic enzymes IDH1/IDH2 produce the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate, which inhibits TET and histone demethylases and causes a hypermethylation phenotype in glioma and leukaemia. In one example, a genomic variant changes a metabolite, the metabolite rewires the epigenome, and the epigenome alters the transcriptome. Multi-omics integration exists to trace chains like this. Key idea The correlation between mRNA and protein levels across genes in a steady-state cell is moderate (often around 0.4–0.6 in mammalian datasets), and across samples for the same gene it is often weaker. Translation rates, protein degradation and time delays explain much of the gap. Do not use a transcriptome as a stand-in for the proteome without checking. See How the layers talk . Exceptions and extensions worth knowing Reverse transcription : retroviruses (HIV), LINE-1 retrotransposons and telomerase write RNA back into DNA. Processed pseudogenes in the genome are fossils of this. RNA-dependent RNA synthesis : RNA viruses (SARS-CoV-2, influenza) replicate RNA directly. Prions : misfolded proteins template the misfolding of others. This passes conformation, not sequence, so it does not violate the dogma. Non-coding RNAs as end products : rRNA, tRNA, snRNA, miRNA and lncRNA function without being translated. Small ORFs and micropeptides : some \"non-coding\" RNAs encode short functional peptides that Ribo-seq and proteomics are still uncovering. Epigenetic inheritance : methylation and chromatin states can be transmitted through cell divisions without changes in DNA sequence."}, {"id": "splicing", "title": "Splicing & isoforms", "text": "Foundations Splicing: one gene, many messages Splicing removes introns from pre-mRNA and joins exons. Alternative splicing lets roughly 20,000 genes produce well over 100,000 transcript isoforms. It is also one of the most underestimated routes by which mutations cause disease. The splicing code in the sequence The spliceosome recognises four short sequence elements in each intron: Exon 1 Exon 2 MAG|GURAGU 5′ splice site (donor) U1 snRNP binds YNYURAY branch point (A) SF1 → U2 snRNP (Y)n polypyrimidine tract U2AF65 binds YAG|G 3′ splice site (acceptor) U2AF35 binds AG R = A/G · Y = C/U · M = A/C · | = exon–intron boundary · ~99% of introns are GU–AG (U2-type); ~0.3% are AU–AC (U12-type minor spliceosome) Fig. 3.1 · Consensus elements of a U2-type intron. The invariant GU and AG dinucleotides are the \"canonical\" splice site positions (±1, ±2) that every variant annotation tool flags. The core motifs are short and degenerate, so they carry too little information on their own to define exons. Additional signals help: exonic and intronic splicing enhancers and silencers (ESE, ESS, ISE, ISS) recruit activating SR proteins (SRSF1–12) or repressing hnRNPs . Tissue-specific regulators (RBFOX, NOVA, PTBP1/2, MBNL, CELF, ESRP) create neural, muscle or epithelial splicing programmes. Chromatin and Pol II elongation speed matter too: slow elongation gives weak exons more time to be recognised. The spliceosome's two chemical steps Branching : the 2′-OH of the branch point adenosine attacks the 5′ splice site, freeing exon 1 and forming a lariat intron. Exon ligation : the free 3′-OH of exon 1 attacks the 3′ splice site, joining the exons and releasing the lariat, which is debranched and degraded. The spliceosome assembles stepwise from five small nuclear ribonucleoproteins (U1, U2, U4, U5, U6) and more than 150 proteins. Exon junction complexes (EJCs) are deposited ~20–24 nt upstream of each junction, which links splicing to mRNA export, translation and nonsense-mediated decay. Types of alternative splicing Fig. 3.2 · Event types. Arcs above the line show one outcome, arcs below show the alternative. Event-based tools (rMATS, SUPPA2, MAJIQ, Whippet) report these as \"percent spliced in\" (PSI, Ψ): the fraction of transcripts that include the variable segment. Event What happens Share in human tissues Example Cassette exon (skipping) A whole exon is included or skipped Most common class in mammals SMN2 exon 7 skipping in spinal muscular atrophy Alternative 5′ splice site Donor moves, changing the end of an exon Common BCL2L1 : Bcl-xL (anti-apoptotic) vs Bcl-xS (pro-apoptotic) Alternative 3′ splice site Acceptor moves, changing the start of an exon Common; cryptic 3′ss usage is the hallmark of SF3B1 -mutant cancers SF3B1 hotspot mutations in MDS, CLL, uveal melanoma Intron retention An intron stays in the mature RNA Frequent in plants; regulated in mammalian neurons and granulocytes Detained introns that regulate gene output during differentiation Mutually exclusive exons Exactly one of two exons is included Less common PKM : PKM1 vs PKM2 (Warburg metabolism) Alternative first exon Different promoters, different first exons Very common; driven by transcription, not the spliceosome Tissue-specific promoters of many genes Alternative last exon / APA Different terminal exons or poly(A) sites ~70% of genes use more than one poly(A) site Short 3′UTRs escape microRNA repression in proliferating cells Back-splicing A downstream donor joins an upstream acceptor, making a circular RNA Thousands of circRNAs, mostly low abundance CDR1as , a microRNA sponge Consequences: nonsense-mediated decay and protein diversity When an isoform contains a premature termination codon (PTC) more than ~50–55 nt upstream of the last exon–exon junction, a downstream EJC remains on the mRNA after the first round of translation and triggers nonsense-mediated decay (NMD) . Cells use this deliberately: \"poison exons\" containing PTCs are included to downregulate genes such as splicing factors themselves (alternative splicing coupled to NMD, AS-NMD). In analysis, NMD means a frameshifting or exon-skipping variant often reduces RNA abundance rather than producing a truncated protein. This is why allele-specific expression in RNA-seq is used to confirm loss-of-function variants. Isoforms that do get translated can differ in domains, localisation signals or interaction surfaces, sometimes with opposite functions (Bcl-xL vs Bcl-xS). How many alternative isoforms produce stable, functional proteins remains an open question; mass spectrometry detects far fewer protein isoforms than RNA-seq predicts, partly because of sensitivity and partly because many isoforms may be noise. Why splicing mutations matter Estimates vary by method, but a substantial fraction of disease-causing variants act through splicing, from roughly 10% when only canonical ±1/±2 sites are counted to well over 20% when deep intronic and exonic splicing variants are included. Mechanisms: Canonical site loss (GT→AT at +1): usually exon skipping or intron retention, often leading to NMD. Cryptic site activation : a variant creates a new donor or acceptor deep in an intron, adding a pseudo-exon (common in CFTR , DMD , ATM ). Exonic variants that disrupt ESEs/ESSs : a \"synonymous\" change can alter splicing. Synonymous does not mean silent. Branch point and polypyrimidine tract variants , which are often missed by tools that only examine ±8 nt from exon boundaries. Trans-acting mutations in spliceosome genes ( SF3B1 , SRSF2 , U2AF1 , ZRSR2 ) in myelodysplastic syndromes and other cancers alter thousands of events at once. Repeat expansions that sequester splicing regulators, as CUG repeats in DMPK trap MBNL in myotonic dystrophy type 1. Example: splicing as a drug target In spinal muscular atrophy, SMN1 is lost and the near-identical SMN2 mostly skips exon 7 because of a single C→T change that weakens an enhancer. The antisense oligonucleotide nusinersen blocks an intronic silencer (ISS-N1) and the small molecule risdiplam stabilises U1 binding at the exon 7 donor. Both restore exon 7 inclusion. These are among the clearest demonstrations that understanding a splicing mechanism at nucleotide resolution can lead to a therapy. Computational analysis of splicing From RNA-seq Splice-aware alignment (STAR, HISAT2) finds reads that span junctions. Use two-pass mode for novel junctions. Long reads (minimap2 -ax splice ) capture whole isoforms. Event-level quantification : rMATS, SUPPA2, MAJIQ, Whippet and VAST-TOOLS compute PSI and test for differential splicing. Annotation-free intron clustering : LeafCutter groups introns that share splice sites and tests intron-usage ratios; widely used for sQTL mapping in GTEx. Exon-bin or isoform-level : DEXSeq (differential exon usage), DRIMSeq, satuRn and IsoformSwitchAnalyzeR (functional consequences of isoform switches). Single-cell splicing : full-length protocols (Smart-seq2/3) or long-read single cell; 3′-tag 10x data are largely blind to internal splicing. From DNA sequence (variant effect prediction) SpliceAI (deep residual CNN over 10 kb context) predicts donor/acceptor gain and loss. Delta score ≥ 0.2 is a common screening threshold and ≥ 0.5 is high-confidence. Pangolin (tissue-aware), MMSplice (modular), SQUIRLS , SPiP , MaxEntScan (classic position-weight/maximum-entropy scorer). Clinical laboratories increasingly validate predicted splice variants with RNA-seq from blood or fibroblasts, or with minigene assays, before classification. Benchmark Systematic comparisons of differential splicing tools report low overlap between tools and strong dependence on read depth, replicate number and event type ( Mehmood et al. 2020, Briefings in Bioinformatics ). For splice-variant prediction, SpliceAI and related deep models are consistently among the top performers on clinical and MPRA-based sets, while older motif scorers lose accuracy outside canonical positions. Results depend on how far into the intron variants lie. Research gap Linking isoforms to function: which of the thousands of detected isoforms are translated and matter? Cell-type-resolved splicing from droplet single-cell data, where coverage of internal exons is poor. Predicting the quantitative effect of a variant (ΔPSI) in a specific tissue, not just whether a site is lost or gained. Calibrated splice predictions for clinical use, especially deep intronic and branch-point variants."}, {"id": "mutations", "title": "Mutations & variation", "text": "Foundations Mutations and genetic variation A mutation matters because of where in the information flow it acts. The same single-base change can do nothing, break a protein, shift a splice site, disable an enhancer or change a drug's metabolism. This chapter classifies variants and traces their consequences through every layer. Terms that are often confused Mutation A change in DNA sequence relative to a parent cell or organism; an event. Variant A position where an individual differs from a reference sequence. Preferred neutral term in clinical genetics. Polymorphism A variant common in a population (conventionally minor allele frequency ≥ 1%). Allele One version of a sequence at a locus. Humans are diploid: two alleles per autosomal locus. Genotype / haplotype The pair of alleles at a locus / the set of alleles on one chromosome copy. Phasing assigns alleles to haplotypes. Germline / somatic Inherited and present in every cell / acquired after conception and present only in a cell lineage (tumours, clonal haematopoiesis, mosaicism). Zygosity Heterozygous (one copy), homozygous (two), hemizygous (one copy present, e.g. X in males), compound heterozygous (two different variants on different copies). Variant classes by size Class Size Typical detection Notes SNV / SNP 1 bp Short-read WGS/WES; arrays for known SNPs ~4–5 million per genome vs reference. Transitions (A↔G, C↔T) outnumber transversions ~2:1; the Ti/Tv ratio is a standard QC metric (~2.0–2.1 genome-wide, ~3.0 in exomes). Indel 1–49 bp Short reads; harder in homopolymers and tandem repeats In coding regions, indels not divisible by 3 cause frameshifts. MNV 2+ adjacent bases Phasing-aware callers Annotating adjacent SNVs separately can give the wrong amino acid. Structural variant (SV) ≥ 50 bp Long reads best; short-read split/discordant reads and depth Deletions, duplications, insertions, inversions, translocations. ~25,000 SVs per genome; they affect more base pairs than SNVs. Copy-number variant (CNV) kb–Mb Read depth, SNP arrays (B-allele frequency), aCGH Recurrent CNVs at 22q11.2, 16p11.2 and 1q21.1 cause neurodevelopmental disorders. Somatic arm-level CNAs are typical of cancer. Short tandem repeat expansion variable ExpansionHunter, long reads Huntington (CAG in HTT ), fragile X (CGG in FMR1 ), C9orf72 ALS/FTD (GGGGCC). Aneuploidy / chromothripsis chromosome Karyotype, depth, SNP arrays Trisomy 21; catastrophic shattering and reassembly of chromosomes in cancer. Mitochondrial variant 1 bp+ Deep sequencing; heteroplasmy-aware callers (Mutect2 mito mode) Disease appears above a heteroplasmy threshold (e.g. m.3243A>G in MELAS). Coding consequences Using the reference A T G G C C T G G A A G (Met-Ala-Trp-Lys): Consequence Change Effect Typical impact Synonymous GCC→GCT (Ala→Ala) Same amino acid Usually neutral, but can disrupt splicing enhancers, codon-optimality or mRNA structure. Missense GCC→ACC (Ala→Thr) Amino-acid substitution From harmless to severe depending on position, chemistry and conservation. The hardest class to interpret. Nonsense (stop-gain) TGG→TGA (Trp→Stop) Premature stop Usually loss of function via NMD; escapes NMD in the last exon. Frameshift GCC→GC- (1-bp del) Reading frame shifts Downstream sequence scrambled, usually followed by a premature stop. In-frame indel −GCC Amino acid deleted e.g. CFTR p.Phe508del, the most common cystic fibrosis variant. Start-loss / stop-loss ATG→ATA / TAA→CAA No initiation / read-through into 3′UTR Loss of protein or extended protein. Splice region GT→GC at +2 See splicing Exon skipping, intron retention, cryptic sites. Variants are written in HGVS nomenclature : NM_000546.6:c.743G>A (cDNA position on a specific transcript) and p.(Arg248Gln) (protein). Always specify the transcript. MANE Select transcripts (agreed by NCBI and EBI) are now the default for clinical reporting. Non-coding consequences Promoter : TERT promoter mutations (C228T, C250T) create ETS-factor binding sites and reactivate telomerase in melanoma, glioblastoma and bladder cancer. Enhancer : a variant in the ZRS enhancer, ~1 Mb from SHH , causes preaxial polydactyly. The FTO obesity locus acts through enhancers that regulate IRX3 / IRX5 , not FTO itself. UTRs : variants that create upstream start codons, or that destroy microRNA sites or polyadenylation signals. Structural and 3D : deleting a TAD boundary lets enhancers reach the wrong gene (enhancer hijacking). Non-coding RNA genes : variants in RNU4ATAC (minor spliceosome snRNA) cause developmental disorders; a recurrent RNU4-2 variant is a recently described common cause of neurodevelopmental disorder. How one variant propagates through the omics layers Layer What the variant can change Assay that detects it QTL type Genome The sequence itself; copy number; structure WGS, WES, arrays, long reads — Epigenome TF binding, CpG creation or loss, accessibility, looping ATAC-seq, ChIP-seq, bisulfite or nanopore methylation, Hi-C caQTL, meQTL (mQTL), hQTL Transcriptome Expression level, splicing, polyadenylation, editing, allelic imbalance RNA-seq, long-read RNA-seq, scRNA-seq eQTL, sQTL, apaQTL, edQTL Proteome Abundance, structure, stability, PTM sites, interactions Mass spectrometry, Olink, SomaScan, structure prediction pQTL, ptmQTL Metabolome Enzyme activity, transporter function, substrate levels LC-MS, GC-MS, NMR mQTL (metabolite QTL) Phenome Disease risk, drug response, quantitative traits Clinical data, biobanks, imaging GWAS, PheWAS Worked example: lactase persistence The variant rs4988235 (−13910 C>T) sits in an intron of MCM6 , ~14 kb upstream of LCT . The T allele creates an OCT1 binding site in an enhancer (epigenome), keeps LCT transcription active into adulthood (transcriptome), maintains lactase enzyme in the intestinal brush border (proteome), allows lactose digestion into glucose and galactose (metabolome), and produces lactose tolerance (phenome). It was strongly selected in populations with a history of dairying. One variant, six layers. Somatic mutations and cancer A tumour is a clonal expansion carrying driver mutations, which confer a growth advantage, and many more passenger mutations, which are neutral hitchhikers. Analyses specific to somatic data include: Tumour–normal paired calling to remove germline variants (Mutect2, Strelka2), with panels of normals to remove recurrent artefacts. Variant allele fraction (VAF) adjusted for purity and copy number to infer clonality and reconstruct phylogenies (PyClone-VI, SciClone, PhyloWGS). Driver discovery through recurrence beyond the background mutation rate (MutSigCV, dNdScv, OncodriveFML), which must correct for replication timing, expression and local sequence context. Mutational signatures : the 96-channel SBS spectrum (6 substitution types × 16 trinucleotide contexts) is decomposed into signatures with known causes. Examples: SBS4 (tobacco smoking, C>A), SBS7 (UV light, C>T at dipyrimidines), SBS2/13 (APOBEC), SBS3 (homologous-recombination deficiency, relevant for PARP inhibitors), SBS1 (clock-like deamination of 5-methylcytosine). Tools: SigProfiler, deconstructSigs, MutationalPatterns, SigLASSO. Tumour mutational burden (TMB) and microsatellite instability (MSI) as biomarkers for immunotherapy; HLA typing and neoantigen prediction (NetMHCpan, pVACtools). Interpreting a germline variant The ACMG/AMP 2015 guidelines combine evidence types into five classes: pathogenic, likely pathogenic, variant of uncertain significance (VUS), likely benign, benign . Evidence types include population frequency (gnomAD; a variant too common for the disease prevalence is benign), computational predictions, functional data, segregation in families, de novo status, and the gene–disease relationship (ClinGen). ClinGen's 2022 calibration of computational predictors (Pejaver et al.) specifies score thresholds for tools such as REVEL and BayesDel so they can count as supporting, moderate or strong evidence. Missense predictors SIFT and PolyPhen-2 (classic), CADD (genome-wide integrative score), REVEL and BayesDel (ensembles), PrimateAI-3D, EVE and ESM-1v (evolutionary and protein language models, trained without clinical labels), AlphaMissense (AlphaFold-derived, 2023). Constraint metrics pLI and LOEUF (gnomAD) measure how strongly a gene tolerates loss-of-function variants. Missense constraint (MPC) and regional constraint highlight intolerant domains. GERP, phyloP and phastCons measure evolutionary conservation per base. Experimental maps Deep mutational scanning (DMS) and saturation genome editing (SGE; e.g. BRCA1 , Findlay et al. 2018) measure the effect of nearly every possible SNV in a region. MaveDB collects these maps, which are increasingly used as functional evidence. Pitfall Predictors trained on ClinVar and evaluated on ClinVar look better than they are, because the same variants or genes leak between training and test sets and because clinical classifications already used older predictors ( Grimm et al. 2015, Human Mutation ). Prefer evaluations on deep mutational scanning data or on newly classified variants. Pharmacogenomics Germline variants in drug-metabolising enzymes and transporters change drug exposure. Examples with clinical guidelines (CPIC, DPWG): CYP2D6 (codeine, tamoxifen; star-allele haplotypes including gene deletions, duplications and hybrids), CYP2C19 (clopidogrel), TPMT / NUDT15 (thiopurines), DPYD (fluoropyrimidines), HLA-B*57:01 (abacavir hypersensitivity), SLCO1B1 (statin myopathy). Calling star alleles requires specialised tools (Aldy, Stargazer, PharmCAT, Cyrius) because of paralogs and structural variants. Population genetics you will need Allele frequency and Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium : strong deviations in controls often indicate genotyping error. Linkage disequilibrium (LD) : nearby variants are inherited together, so a GWAS signal marks a region, not a causal variant. Fine-mapping (SuSiE, FINEMAP) assigns posterior probabilities to variants within the region. Population structure and ancestry : control with principal components or mixed models, otherwise allele-frequency differences between groups create false associations. About 85% or more of GWAS participants have been of European ancestry, which reduces the portability of polygenic scores to other groups. Effect size versus frequency : rare variants can have large effects (Mendelian disease); common variants usually have small effects (complex traits). Both contribute to heritability. Research gap Most VUSs are missense or non-coding. Accurate, calibrated prediction for non-coding variants is largely unsolved. Cell-type- and context-specific variant effects, including variants that act only under stimulation or at particular developmental stages. Equitable references: allele frequencies, LD panels and predictor training data remain skewed toward European ancestry. Somatic mosaicism in normal tissues: detecting variants at VAF below 1% without error-corrected sequencing."}, {"id": "omes", "title": "Map of the -omes", "text": "The layers A map of the -omes \"-omics\" means measuring all, or nearly all, members of a class of molecules at once, without choosing them in advance. That shift from hypothesis-first to measure-everything changes the statistics: you test thousands of features with few samples, so multiple testing, normalisation and study design dominate. The full list Omics What is measured Main technologies Typical features per sample Core question DNA level Genomics DNA sequence and variation Short-read WGS/WES (Illumina, Element, Ultima, MGI), long reads (PacBio HiFi, Oxford Nanopore), SNP arrays ~5M SNVs/indels, ~25k SVs Which variants does this genome carry, and which matter? Metagenomics All DNA in an environmental or host sample 16S/ITS amplicons, shotgun sequencing, long reads 10 2 –10 4 taxa Who is there and what can they do? Pan-genomics Sequence diversity across many genomes of a species Many de novo assemblies, graph genomes graph nodes/edges What does one reference miss? Epigenomics DNA methylation, histone marks, accessibility, TF binding, 3D structure WGBS, EM-seq, arrays, nanopore 5mC; ChIP-seq, CUT&Tag, ATAC-seq, DNase-seq; Hi-C, Micro-C ~28M CpGs; 10 4 –10 5 peaks Which parts of the genome are readable here, and why? RNA level Transcriptomics RNA abundance and structure (isoforms) Bulk RNA-seq, long-read RNA-seq, microarrays (legacy), qPCR panels ~20k genes, ~100k+ transcripts Which genes are used, how much, in which isoform? Single-cell transcriptomics RNA per individual cell or nucleus 10x Chromium, combinatorial indexing (Parse, Scale), Smart-seq3, BD Rhapsody 10 3 –10 6 cells × 20k genes Which cell types and states exist, and how do they change? Spatial transcriptomics RNA with tissue coordinates Visium/Visium HD, Stereo-seq, Slide-seq; imaging: Xenium, MERFISH/MERSCOPE, CosMx, seqFISH 10 4 –10 7 spots or cells Where are cell states, and who are their neighbours? Epitranscriptomics RNA modifications (m6A, Ψ, m5C, A-to-I) MeRIP-seq, miCLIP, GLORI, direct RNA nanopore 10 4 sites How are RNA fate and translation tuned chemically? Translatomics Ribosome-bound mRNA Ribo-seq, polysome profiling, TRAP ~10k translated ORFs What is actually being translated, and where do ribosomes start? Interactomics (RNA) RNA–protein and RNA–RNA contacts CLIP-seq (eCLIP, iCLIP), PARIS, RIC binding sites Which proteins regulate which RNAs? Protein level Proteomics Protein identity and abundance LC-MS/MS (DDA, DIA, TMT), affinity (Olink, SomaScan), antibody arrays; emerging single-molecule sequencing 3k–12k proteins (cells); 1k–11k (plasma, affinity) Which proteins are present, how much, in what form? PTM-omics Phosphorylation, ubiquitination, acetylation, glycosylation Enrichment + MS (TiO 2 , IMAC, antibody pulldown) 10 4 –10 5 sites Which pathways are switched on? Structural proteomics 3D structure and conformations Cryo-EM, X-ray, XL-MS, HDX-MS, limited proteolysis; AlphaFold predictions structures How does shape explain function and variant effects? Interactomics (protein) Protein–protein interactions and complexes AP-MS, BioID/TurboID proximity labelling, Y2H, co-fractionation 10 4 –10 5 interactions Which machines do proteins form? Immunomics / immunopeptidomics TCR/BCR repertoires; HLA-presented peptides AIRR-seq, single-cell VDJ, HLA peptide MS 10 5 –10 7 clonotypes What does the immune system see and remember? Glycomics Glycans and glycoproteins MS, HPLC, lectin arrays 10 2 –10 3 glycans How do sugar chains modulate function and recognition? Small-molecule level Metabolomics Small molecules LC-MS, GC-MS, NMR, CE-MS, MS imaging 10 2 (targeted) to 10 4 features (untargeted) What is the biochemical state right now? Lipidomics Lipid species Shotgun or LC-MS lipidomics 10 2 –10 3 lipids How are membranes, signalling and energy storage changing? Fluxomics Reaction rates Stable-isotope tracing ( 13 C, 15 N) + MS/NMR, flux balance models 10 1 –10 3 fluxes How fast is material moving through pathways? Exposomics Environmental exposures over life High-resolution MS of blood/urine, sensors, questionnaires 10 3 –10 5 features What has this person been exposed to? Organism and clinic Phenomics Systematic phenotypes EHRs, HPO terms, wearables, high-content imaging, Cell Painting 10 2 –10 3 traits How do we describe outcomes rigorously? Radiomics / pathomics Quantitative image features CT, MRI, PET; whole-slide histology + deep learning 10 2 –10 3 features Can images stand in for, or add to, molecular profiles? Pharmacogenomics Genetic determinants of drug response Genotyping panels, WGS, star-allele calling dozens of genes Which drug and dose for this genotype? Liquid biopsy / fragmentomics Cell-free DNA/RNA in blood Deep targeted sequencing, low-pass WGS, cfDNA methylation fragments, VAFs, methylation Can we detect and track disease from blood? Three properties that shape every analysis p ≫ n Far more features (p) than samples (n). Classical regression breaks down; you need regularisation, dimensionality reduction, empirical Bayes shrinkage and false discovery rate (FDR) control (Benjamini–Hochberg, Storey's q-value). Measurements are relative Sequencing returns a fixed number of reads per library, so counts are compositional: one feature rising forces others down. Normalisation (size factors, TMM, CLR) tries to recover comparable scales. Spike-ins can restore absolute scale. Technical variation is large Batch, operator, reagent lot, run date and sample handling can explain more variance than biology. Randomise, block and record; correct afterwards only when the design allows it. The generic analysis arc Every layer follows a similar arc, even though the tools differ: Design : question, replicates, power, randomisation, controls, metadata standards. Primary processing : instrument signal to reads or spectra; QC (FastQC, MultiQC; PTXQC for proteomics). Secondary processing : reads to features (alignment, quantification, variant/peak calling, identification). Feature-level QC and normalisation : filter low-quality samples and features, correct library size or loading, examine batch with PCA. Statistical modelling : differential abundance, association, clustering, prediction. Interpretation : annotation, enrichment, networks, integration with other layers and with prior knowledge. Validation and sharing : independent cohort, orthogonal assay, data deposition (GEO, PRIDE, MetaboLights, EGA)."}, {"id": "generation", "title": "How omics data are generated", "text": "From sample to file How omics data are generated Every file you analyse is the end of a physical process: a sample was collected, molecules were extracted, converted into something an instrument can read, and measured. Knowing that process tells you where the artefacts in your data come from and which parameters in your pipeline exist to correct them. The common path Study design Question, groups, replicates, randomisation, metadata sheet Sample collection Blood, tissue, cells, stool, swab; time to freezing; preservative (EDTA, PAXgene, RNAlater, FFPE) Extraction Isolate DNA, RNA, protein or metabolites; measure quantity and integrity (Qubit, RIN/DV200, BCA) Conversion Library preparation for sequencing, digestion for proteomics, derivatisation for GC-MS Measurement Sequencer, mass spectrometer, array scanner, NMR, microscope Raw data FASTQ / POD5 / BAM, RAW / mzML, IDAT, FID, images QC & deposit Run metrics, sample QC, upload to GEO/SRA/PRIDE/MetaboLights Pre-analytical variation Many apparent biological signals come from handling. RNA degrades within minutes at room temperature; hypoxia during surgery changes gene expression; metabolites keep changing in unfrozen blood; formalin fixation damages DNA (C>T artefacts) and fragments RNA. Record collection time, processing delay, freeze–thaw cycles and storage duration for every sample, and keep them balanced across groups. Sequencing libraries A sequencing library is a collection of DNA fragments, each flanked by known adapter sequences that let the sequencer capture, amplify and read them. Whatever the starting molecule (genomic DNA, cDNA from RNA, bisulfite-converted DNA, immunoprecipitated chromatin, Tn5-tagged fragments), the result has the same structure: Fragmentation : sonication, enzymatic shearing or transposase (tagmentation) produces fragments of ~200–600 bp for short reads. Long-read libraries keep fragments of 10–100 kb or longer. End repair, A-tailing and adapter ligation attach adapters; tagmentation (Nextera) fragments and adds adapters in one step. PCR amplification adds index sequences and enriches adapter-ligated fragments. PCR creates duplicates and GC bias; PCR-free WGS avoids this when input DNA allows. Unique molecular identifiers (UMIs) : random barcodes attached before amplification, so reads from the same original molecule can be collapsed. Essential for single-cell counting and ultra-low-frequency variant detection. Target enrichment (exomes, panels): hybridisation capture with biotinylated probes or amplicon PCR. Coverage then depends on probe efficiency, which is why exome depth is uneven. Library QC : fragment size (Bioanalyzer/TapeStation), concentration (qPCR) and adapter-dimer contamination. Short-read sequencing by synthesis (Illumina) Library fragments bind to a flow cell and are copied in place into clusters of ~1,000 identical molecules (bridge amplification or exclusion amplification on patterned flow cells). Then sequencing proceeds in cycles. In each cycle a polymerase adds one fluorescently labelled, reversibly terminated nucleotide to every cluster; a camera images the flow cell; the dye and terminator are cleaved; the next cycle begins. The colour of each cluster in each cycle is the base. Errors grow along the read because clusters lose synchrony (phasing): some molecules fall a base behind or jump ahead, blurring the signal. That is why base quality drops toward the 3′ end of reads. The instrument converts intensities into base calls and Phred quality scores, stored in FASTQ files: Long-read sequencing PacBio HiFi (SMRT) A single polymerase copies a circularised DNA molecule (SMRTbell) inside a tiny well (zero-mode waveguide) while fluorescent nucleotides are detected in real time. Because the molecule is circular, the polymerase reads it many times; a consensus of these passes gives 15–25 kb reads at ~99.9% accuracy. Polymerase kinetics (pauses) reveal methylation. Oxford Nanopore A motor protein ratchets a single DNA or RNA strand through a protein pore in a membrane. Ionic current through the pore depends on the ~5–9 bases inside it at each moment. A neural-network basecaller translates the current trace (the \"squiggle\") into bases, and modified bases (5mC, 6mA, m6A) change the current, so they are detected directly. Read length is limited only by the molecule. Arrays Microarrays measure many known targets at once by hybridising labelled sample to millions of immobilised probes. SNP genotyping arrays (e.g. Illumina Global Screening Array, UK Biobank Axiom) read the two alleles of ~0.6–2 million variants from two colour channels; the ratio gives the genotype (plotted as genotype clusters) and the B-allele frequency also reveals copy number. Methylation arrays (EPIC) read bisulfite-converted DNA at ~935k CpGs. Expression arrays are now largely replaced by RNA-seq, but decades of array data remain in GEO. Mass spectrometry Proteomics, metabolomics and lipidomics are mostly measured by mass spectrometry (MS). Molecules are separated by liquid or gas chromatography, ionised (electrospray for LC, electron ionisation for GC, MALDI for imaging), then the mass analyser separates ions by mass-to-charge ratio (m/z): Analyser Principle Resolution Typical use Quadrupole (Q, QqQ) Oscillating electric fields let only one m/z pass at a time Low Targeted quantification (MRM), filtering Time-of-flight (TOF) Lighter ions fly faster down a flight tube High, fast Untargeted metabolomics, timsTOF proteomics with ion mobility Orbitrap Ions orbit an electrode; frequency of oscillation gives m/z via Fourier transform Very high Proteomics (Exploris, Astral), metabolomics FT-ICR Cyclotron frequency in a strong magnet Highest Complex mixtures, top-down Ion mobility (IMS) Separates ions by shape and size in a gas Extra dimension Isomer separation, collision cross section In tandem MS (MS/MS), a selected ion is fragmented by collision with gas and the fragments are measured. Fragment patterns identify peptide sequences and small-molecule structures. Raw files are vendor-specific (.raw, .d, .wiff) and are converted to open mzML for most open-source tools. NMR, imaging and other readouts NMR spectroscopy measures how atomic nuclei (¹H, ¹³C) in a magnetic field absorb radio-frequency energy; chemical shifts identify functional groups. Quantitative and reproducible, used in large cohort metabolomics. Imaging-based spatial omics (MERFISH, Xenium, CosMx) hybridise fluorescent probes in repeated rounds; each transcript's colour sequence across rounds forms a barcode decoded to a gene. Protein imaging (CODEX, IMC) uses barcoded or metal-tagged antibodies. Flow and mass cytometry (CyTOF) measure ~20–50 proteins per cell in millions of cells. Affinity proteomics (Olink, SomaScan) converts protein binding into DNA barcodes or aptamer signals read by sequencing or arrays. Quality metrics at the source Data type Metric What good looks like Any sequencing % bases ≥ Q30; adapter content; duplication >80–90% Q30 on modern Illumina; low adapter dimers WGS Mean depth, % genome ≥ 20×, insert size, contamination (VerifyBamID) ~30× germline; contamination <2% RNA-seq RIN/DV200, % rRNA, % uniquely mapped, 3′ bias RIN ≥ 7 for poly(A); >70–80% unique mapping scRNA-seq Cells recovered, median genes/cell, % reads in cells, % mitochondrial Knee plot with clear separation of cells from empty droplets ATAC-seq TSS enrichment, fragment-size periodicity, FRiP TSS enrichment > ~6–7 (ENCODE); visible nucleosome ladder Proteomics IDs per run, missed cleavages, mass error, RT stability Stable IDs in QC standard (e.g. HeLa digest) across runs Metabolomics Pooled-QC CV, internal standard RT/intensity drift, blanks CV < 20–30% in pooled QCs"}, {"id": "genomics", "title": "Genomics", "text": "DNA layer Genomics Reading DNA and finding where it differs. Germline genomics asks which inherited variants explain disease or traits; somatic genomics asks which acquired variants drive cancer and clonal expansion; assembly asks what the genome actually looks like when no reference fits. Research questions Which variant explains this patient's rare disease? (diagnostic yield of WES is ~25–40%, WGS adds several percent more) Which loci are associated with a complex trait, and which variant and gene in each locus is causal? How much heritability do rare and structural variants explain beyond common SNPs? Which somatic mutations drive a tumour, in what order, and which therapies do they predict? What is the full structural diversity of human genomes across ancestries (pangenome)? Can polygenic scores predict risk equally well across populations? Technologies Platform Read length Accuracy Strengths Weaknesses Illumina SBS 2×150 bp >99.9% (Q30+) Cheapest per base, mature tooling, the reference standard for SNVs Repeats, SVs, phasing, GC bias Element AVITI, MGI/Complete Genomics, Ultima 150–300 bp Q30–Q40+ Competitive cost and accuracy Similar short-read limits; Ultima has homopolymer-specific errors PacBio HiFi 15–25 kb ~Q30 (99.9%) Accurate long reads: SVs, phasing, repeats, assembly, native 5mC Higher cost per sample Oxford Nanopore 10 kb to >1 Mb ~Q20+ with recent chemistry and basecallers Ultra-long reads, real-time, portable, native base modifications, direct RNA Systematic errors in homopolymers; basecaller version matters Linked/optical (Bionano) >150 kb molecules n/a Large SVs, scaffolding No base-level sequence SNP arrays n/a High for common SNPs Very cheap; basis of biobank GWAS with imputation Only known variants; rare variants are poorly imputed How genomic data are generated Sample Blood (EDTA), saliva, buccal swab, fresh-frozen or FFPE tissue; tumour plus matched normal for somatic work DNA extraction Silica columns or magnetic beads; high-molecular-weight kits (>50 kb) for long reads DNA QC Concentration (Qubit), purity (A260/280 ≈ 1.8), integrity (DIN, pulsed-field gel) Library PCR-free WGS; exome capture with probes (~35–60 Mb); amplicon panels; size selection for HiFi/ONT Sequencing 2 × 150 bp to ~30× (germline) or 60–100× tumour; HiFi 15–25 kb; ONT ultra-long Raw output FASTQ (short reads), unaligned BAM (PacBio), POD5 signal (ONT) Arrays follow a different route: DNA is amplified, fragmented, hybridised to probes on a BeadChip and scanned into IDAT intensity files, then genotypes are called from the two allele intensities (GenomeStudio, gtc2vcf) and missing variants are imputed. Reading aligned sequences After alignment, every read sits at its position on the reference. A genome browser such as IGV shows this as a pileup : bases that match the reference are drawn as plain bars, and only differences are shown. Real variants appear in a consistent fraction of reads from both strands; sequencing errors appear in single reads at low quality. A caller summarises this evidence as one line of a VCF file: #CHROM POS ID REF ALT QUAL FILTER INFO FORMAT SAMPLE chr2 21012345 . C T 812 PASS DP=41;AF=0.5 GT:AD:DP:GQ 0/1:21,20:41:99 │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ │ └ genotype 0/1 = heterozygous; 21 ref and 20 alt reads; depth 41; genotype quality 99 │ │ │ │ │ │ │ └ site-level annotations └ keys for the sample column │ │ │ │ │ │ └ passed all filters │ │ │ │ │ └ Phred-scaled confidence that a variant exists │ │ │ │ └ alternative allele └ reference allele │ │ │ └ dbSNP identifier (rs…), or \".\" if none └ chromosome and 1-based position (GRCh38) The germline short-read pipeline QC and trimming (FastQC, fastp): adapters, quality, duplication. Alignment to GRCh38 (BWA-MEM2; minimap2 for long reads). Uses the Burrows–Wheeler transform/FM-index to find exact seed matches quickly, then Smith–Waterman local alignment. Output: SAM/BAM/CRAM with mapping quality (MAPQ = −10 log 10 P(mapped position wrong)). Duplicate marking (Picard, samblaster, GATK MarkDuplicatesSpark): PCR and optical duplicates inflate confidence. Base quality score recalibration (BQSR): models systematic quality errors by machine cycle and context. Less important with modern instruments and with DeepVariant. Variant calling : GATK HaplotypeCaller re-assembles local haplotypes with a de Bruijn graph and computes genotype likelihoods with a pair-HMM; DeepVariant encodes read pileups as images and classifies genotypes with a CNN; Strelka2, FreeBayes, Octopus and DRAGEN are alternatives. Cohort calling uses gVCFs and joint genotyping (GATK GenotypeGVCFs, GLnexus). Filtering : VQSR (Gaussian mixture on annotations) for large cohorts, hard filters for small ones, or learned filters. Annotation : VEP, ANNOVAR, SnpEff add gene, transcript and consequence, population frequency (gnomAD), predictions (CADD, REVEL, SpliceAI, AlphaMissense) and clinical databases (ClinVar). Prioritisation : Exomiser and LIRICAL combine variant scores with patient phenotypes (HPO terms); inheritance-mode filters for trios. Key idea Variant calling is a statistical inference, not a lookup. A genotype call is the most likely genotype given read evidence, base and mapping qualities and a prior. Report and filter on genotype quality (GQ), depth (DP) and allele balance, and use benchmark regions to know where calls can be trusted. Structural variants and copy number Short-read SV callers combine three signals: discordant read pairs (unexpected insert size or orientation), split reads (one read spanning a breakpoint), and read depth . Assembly-based callers reconstruct breakpoint sequence. Manta, DELLY, LUMPY/smoove and GRIDSS2 are widely used, often merged with SURVIVOR or Jasmine. Long-read callers (Sniffles2, cuteSV, pbsv, SVIM) and assembly-based comparison (PAV, SVIM-asm, dipcall) recover far more SVs, especially insertions in repeats. For copy number: CNVkit and GATK gCNV (exomes and panels), CNVnator and Canvas (WGS), ASCAT, FACETS and PURPLE (tumour allele-specific copy number with purity and ploidy), and GISTIC2 (recurrent CNAs across tumours). Somatic variant calling Tumour samples are mixtures of tumour and normal cells, often with subclones, so somatic variants may appear at 5% VAF or lower. Use a matched normal, a panel of normals, and germline resource filtering (Mutect2 with FilterMutectCalls, Strelka2, VarDict, LoFreq). FFPE samples cause C>T deamination artefacts, and oxidation causes G>T artefacts; orientation-bias filters address both. Ultra-sensitive applications (minimal residual disease, liquid biopsy) need unique molecular identifiers (UMIs) and duplex sequencing to suppress errors below 10 −6 . De novo assembly and pangenomes When the reference is incomplete or absent, reads are assembled. Overlap-layout-consensus and string graphs (hifiasm, HiCanu, Flye, Shasta) handle long reads; de Bruijn graphs (SPAdes, MEGAHIT) handle short reads. Hybrid approaches (Verkko) combine HiFi with ultra-long ONT for telomere-to-telomere assemblies; Hi-C or parental data (trio binning) phase haplotypes. Assess with N50, BUSCO completeness, Merqury k-mer QV and Inspector. The Human Pangenome Reference Consortium builds graph references (minigraph-Cactus, PGGB) that reduce reference bias; vg giraffe aligns reads to graphs. Population and statistical genetics GWAS : test each variant for association with a trait while controlling population structure. PLINK 2 for QC and simple models; mixed models handle relatedness and structure at biobank scale (REGENIE, SAIGE, BOLT-LMM, fastGWA). Genome-wide significance is conventionally p < 5×10 −8 . Imputation : infer untyped variants from haplotype reference panels (TOPMed, 1000 Genomes, HRC) with Beagle 5, Minimac4 or IMPUTE5; pre-phasing with SHAPEIT5 or Eagle2. Heritability and genetic correlation : LD score regression (LDSC), GCTA-GREML, S-LDSC for enrichment in annotations (cell-type-specific enhancers). Fine-mapping : SuSiE, FINEMAP, PolyFun (functional priors) output credible sets of likely causal variants. Polygenic scores : PRS-CS, LDpred2, SBayesRC, PRS-CSx (multi-ancestry). Evaluate with incremental R² or AUC in held-out cohorts of each ancestry. Rare-variant burden tests : SKAT-O, ACAT, gene-based burden in exome biobanks. Tools Benchmarks Pitfall Benchmarks such as Genome in a Bottle cover \"high-confidence\" regions, which historically excluded the hardest parts of the genome. A pipeline scoring 99.9% F1 there may do much worse in segmental duplications, the MHC or medically relevant genes with paralogs (e.g. SMN1/2 , PMS2 , CYP2D6 ). Check stratified performance (GIAB stratification BEDs with hap.py or vcfdist), not only the headline number. Research gap Variant calling against pangenome graphs, and benchmarks that represent diverse ancestries. Joint calling of SNVs, SVs, repeat expansions and methylation from one long-read run. Interpretation of non-coding and structural variants in rare disease, where most unsolved cases may sit. Polygenic score portability, calibration and clinical utility across ancestries. Somatic mosaicism and clonal haematopoiesis detection in normal tissues."}, {"id": "epigenomics", "title": "Epigenomics", "text": "DNA layer · regulation Epigenomics Heritable or stable changes in gene activity that do not change DNA sequence: methylation of DNA, chemical marks on histones, which DNA is accessible, where transcription factors sit, and how chromosomes fold in 3D. Epigenomics explains how one genome makes hundreds of cell types. Research questions Which regulatory elements are active in this cell type, and which genes do they control? How do disease-associated variants change TF binding, accessibility or methylation? Does methylation mark disease state, ageing (epigenetic clocks), exposure (smoking) or cell composition? Can methylation patterns in cell-free DNA detect cancer and identify its tissue of origin? How does 3D genome folding (loops, TADs) control enhancer–promoter communication? How epigenomic data are generated Assay Wet-lab steps Input Readout WGBS / EM-seq Extract DNA → bisulfite or enzymatic conversion of unmethylated C → library → sequence 2 × 150 bp to ~30× 10 ng–1 µg DNA C vs T at every CpG Methylation array Bisulfite-convert DNA → amplify → hybridise to EPIC BeadChip → scan ~250–500 ng Methylated / unmethylated intensities (IDAT) ATAC-seq Isolate nuclei → Tn5 tagmentation (cuts open DNA and inserts adapters) → PCR → sequence paired-end 500–50,000 cells Fragments from accessible DNA ChIP-seq Crosslink with formaldehyde → shear chromatin → immunoprecipitate with an antibody → reverse crosslinks → library 10 5 –10 7 cells + input control Fragments bound by the target protein or mark CUT&Tag Permeabilised cells + antibody → protein A–Tn5 binds antibody and tagments in place → PCR 10 3 –10 5 cells Low-background target fragments Hi-C / Micro-C Crosslink → digest (restriction enzyme or MNase) → fill ends with biotin → proximity ligation → shear → pull down junctions → paired-end sequencing 10 5 –10 6 cells Read pairs joining DNA that touched in 3D DNA methylation In mammals methylation is mostly 5-methylcytosine (5mC) at CpG dinucleotides. About 70–80% of CpGs are methylated, except in CpG islands at active promoters. DNMT3A/B establish new methylation, DNMT1 maintains it through replication, and TET enzymes oxidise 5mC to 5hmC, 5fC and 5caC on the way to demethylation. Promoter methylation is associated with silencing; gene-body methylation correlates with active transcription. Assay Principle Coverage Notes WGBS Bisulfite converts unmethylated C→U (read as T); methylated C stays C ~28M CpGs, genome-wide Gold standard; DNA damage; cannot separate 5mC from 5hmC EM-seq Enzymatic conversion (TET2 + APOBEC3A) Genome-wide Less DNA damage, more even coverage than bisulfite RRBS MspI digestion enriches CpG-rich regions, then bisulfite ~2–4M CpGs Cheap; biased to CpG islands Illumina EPIC v2 array Probe hybridisation after bisulfite ~935k CpGs Dominant in epidemiology (EWAS) and clocks; probe cross-reactivity and SNP-in-probe issues Nanopore / PacBio native Detects modified bases from raw signal or polymerase kinetics Genome-wide, long-range, phased Haplotype-resolved methylation; 5hmC on nanopore TAPS, 5-letter/6-base sequencing Chemistries that separate 5mC and 5hmC Genome-wide Newer, growing adoption Analysis : bisulfite-aware alignment (Bismark, bwa-meth, BSBolt) uses three-letter alignment (C/T and G/A converted genomes). Methylation is then extracted per CpG (MethylDackel, Bismark extractor). Arrays are processed with minfi, SeSAMe or ChAMP (normalisation: Noob, BMIQ, functional normalisation). Differential methylation is tested per CpG (methylKit, DSS, limma on M-values) or per region (DMRcate, bsseq/BSmooth, dmrseq, metilene). Because blood and tissue methylation is dominated by cell composition, estimate proportions (Houseman method, EpiDISH, MethylCIBERSORT) and adjust. Epigenetic clocks Penalised regression on methylation predicts age with striking accuracy: Horvath's multi-tissue clock (353 CpGs, 2013) and Hannum's blood clock. Later clocks predict health outcomes rather than chronological age: PhenoAge, GrimAge and the pace-of-ageing measure DunedinPACE. Their biological meaning, and whether interventions that change them change health, are open questions. Chromatin accessibility ATAC-seq uses hyperactive Tn5 transposase to insert sequencing adapters into open chromatin. It needs ~50,000 cells (or single nuclei in scATAC). Fragment lengths show nucleosome-free (<100 bp), mono-nucleosome (~200 bp) and di-nucleosome peaks; the TSS enrichment score and fragment-size periodicity are the main QC metrics. DNase-seq and MNase-seq are older alternatives. Pipeline : trim (Nextera adapters), align (Bowtie2 or BWA), remove mitochondrial reads and duplicates, shift reads +4/−5 bp for Tn5 offset, call peaks (MACS2/3, Genrich, HMMRATAC), build a consensus peak set, count (featureCounts), test differential accessibility (DESeq2/edgeR, DiffBind, csaw). Downstream: motif enrichment (HOMER, MEME-ChIP), TF footprinting (TOBIAS, HINT-ATAC), and per-sample TF activity (chromVAR). Histone marks and transcription factor binding Mark Location Meaning Peak shape H3K4me3 Promoters Active or poised promoter Sharp H3K4me1 Enhancers Enhancer (primed or active) Broad-ish H3K27ac Enhancers, promoters Active element; super-enhancers (ROSE) Sharp to medium H3K36me3 Gene bodies Transcriptional elongation Broad H3K27me3 Developmental genes Polycomb repression; bivalent with H3K4me3 in stem cells Very broad domains H3K9me3 Repeats, heterochromatin Constitutive repression Very broad Assays: ChIP-seq (antibody pulldown of crosslinked chromatin; needs input control), CUT&RUN and CUT&Tag (antibody-tethered nuclease or Tn5 cutting in situ; low background, low cell input, single-cell versions). Peak calling: MACS2/3 for sharp marks and TFs; broad mode, SICER2 or epic2 for broad marks; SEACR for CUT&RUN. Reproducibility between replicates is assessed with IDR (irreproducible discovery rate), as in ENCODE pipelines. ChromHMM and Segway combine many marks into chromatin states (active promoter, strong enhancer, Polycomb-repressed, heterochromatin), as in the Roadmap Epigenomics reference maps. 3D genome Hi-C crosslinks chromatin, digests it, ligates nearby fragments and sequences the junctions, giving a genome-wide contact matrix. Micro-C uses MNase for nucleosome resolution; capture Hi-C, HiChIP and PLAC-seq focus on promoters or protein-anchored contacts. Processing (Juicer, HiC-Pro, distiller, HiCExplorer) produces .hic or .cool/.mcool files, normalised by iterative correction (ICE) or Knight–Ruiz balancing. Features are then called: A/B compartments (eigenvector of the correlation matrix), TADs (insulation score, Arrowhead, TopDom), loops (HiCCUPS, Mustache, chromosight), and differential contacts (diffHic, multiHiCcompare, CHESS). Contact frequency drops with genomic distance, so all comparisons must account for that distance decay. Linking regulatory elements to genes The nearest-gene assumption is wrong for a large share of enhancers. Better approaches use activity and contact together: the Activity-by-Contact (ABC) model (enhancer activity × Hi-C contact), correlations between accessibility and expression across samples or single cells (peak-to-gene links in ArchR, Signac), and CRISPRi screens as ground truth (e.g. ENCODE-rE2G models trained on CRISPR perturbation data). Tools Benchmarks Pitfall In EWAS, smoking, age, sex, genotype (meQTLs) and cell composition can all produce differential methylation. A \"disease signature\" may be a composition signature. Adjust for estimated cell proportions and check whether hits are known meQTLs. Also check causality direction: disease, medication and lifestyle can change methylation rather than the reverse. Research gap Causal link from a specific mark to a specific expression change (most epigenomic data are correlational; epigenome editing with dCas9 fusions is starting to test this). Single-cell multi-modal epigenomics (methylation + accessibility + RNA in the same cell) at scale. Haplotype-resolved methylation from long reads and its role in imprinting and allele-specific expression. Enhancer–gene linking in rare cell types and disease states without CRISPR ground truth. Biological interpretation of epigenetic clocks and their response to interventions."}, {"id": "transcriptomics", "title": "Transcriptomics (bulk)", "text": "RNA layer Transcriptomics Measuring which genes are expressed, how much, and in which isoform. Bulk RNA-seq is the most widely used omics assay in biology, and its statistical tooling is the most mature, so it is the best place to learn the logic that every other layer reuses. Research questions Which genes change between conditions (disease vs control, treated vs untreated, time points)? Which pathways and programmes are active, and which upstream regulators explain them? Which isoforms, fusions, editing sites or allele-specific expression patterns are present? Can expression classify patients (subtypes, prognosis, treatment response)? How do genetic variants affect expression (eQTLs) and splicing (sQTLs)? Experimental choices that decide what you can analyse RNA selection Poly(A)+ captures mature mRNA; rRNA depletion keeps non-polyadenylated and pre-mRNA (intronic reads). Small-RNA libraries for miRNA. Degraded samples (FFPE) need depletion or capture. Strandedness and length Stranded libraries (dUTP) resolve antisense transcription. Paired-end 2×100–150 bp helps isoforms and fusions. 3′-tag methods (QuantSeq, BRB-seq) are cheap for gene counts but blind to isoforms. Depth and replicates ~20–30M reads per sample is typical for gene-level DE; isoform work needs more. Biological replicates matter more than depth: aim for six or more per group when you can. How transcriptomic data are generated Sample & stabilise Snap-freeze, RNAlater or PAXgene (blood) within minutes; RNA degrades fast RNA extraction TRIzol or column kits; DNase treatment removes genomic DNA RNA QC RIN (1–10) or DV200 for FFPE; quantity by Qubit Select RNA Poly(A) capture with oligo-dT beads, or rRNA depletion (keeps non-polyadenylated RNA) Make cDNA Fragment RNA → reverse transcription → second strand with dUTP (marks strand) → adapters → PCR Sequence ~20–40 M read pairs, 2 × 100–150 bp; long-read cDNA or direct RNA for full isoforms The end product of quantification is a count matrix : genes in rows, samples in columns, each entry the number of reads (or estimated fragments) assigned to that gene. Everything downstream works on this table. The standard pipeline QC : FastQC/MultiQC, rRNA content, strand check (RSeQC infer_experiment, salmon's automatic library type), gene body coverage (3′ bias indicates degradation), RIN or DV200 from the lab. Quantification , two routes: Alignment-based : STAR or HISAT2 to the genome, then featureCounts or HTSeq for gene counts; RSEM for isoforms. Gives BAMs for visual inspection, novel junctions and variant calling. Pseudoalignment / selective alignment : salmon or kallisto match reads to transcript sequences and resolve multi-mapping reads with an EM algorithm. Fast and accurate for annotated transcripts; use salmon with a decoy-aware index. Import with tximport/tximeta to gene level. Filtering and normalisation : remove genes with very low counts (edgeR filterByExpr). Between-sample normalisation with DESeq2's median-of-ratios or edgeR's TMM. TPM and FPKM are within-sample units for display; do not feed them into count-based DE models. Exploration : variance-stabilising transform (vst/rlog), PCA, sample-distance heatmaps. Look for outliers, batch effects, sex mismatches ( XIST , chrY genes) and sample swaps. Differential expression : negative binomial GLMs (DESeq2, edgeR) or linear models on log-CPM with precision weights (limma-voom). Include batch and covariates in the design matrix. Use empirical Bayes shrinkage of dispersions and of fold changes (apeglm, ashr) for ranking. Multiple testing : Benjamini–Hochberg FDR. IHW (independent hypothesis weighting) can increase power. Interpretation : over-representation (clusterProfiler, g:Profiler, Enrichr), rank-based GSEA (fgsea, GSEA), sample-level scores (GSVA, ssGSEA, singscore), TF and pathway activity from footprints (DoRothEA/CollecTRI with decoupleR, PROGENy), co-expression modules (WGCNA, hdWGCNA for single-cell). Why the negative binomial Counts from technical replicates are roughly Poisson (variance = mean). Biological replicates vary more, so variance = μ + αμ², where α is the dispersion. With three replicates you cannot estimate α per gene reliably, so DESeq2 and edgeR borrow information across genes: they shrink each gene's dispersion toward a trend fitted against mean expression. That shrinkage is the main reason these tools work with small sample sizes. Beyond gene counts Isoforms : short-read isoform quantification is uncertain when isoforms share most exons. Long-read RNA-seq (PacBio Kinnex/Iso-Seq, ONT cDNA or direct RNA) sequences full transcripts. Tools: IsoQuant, Bambu, FLAIR, StringTie2, TALON, ESPRESSO; SQANTI3 classifies and filters isoforms against the annotation. Splicing : see the splicing chapter (rMATS, LeafCutter, MAJIQ, SUPPA2). Fusion genes : STAR-Fusion, Arriba, FusionCatcher; important in leukaemia (BCR::ABL1), sarcomas and prostate cancer (TMPRSS2::ERG). Allele-specific expression : phASER, GATK ASEReadCounter; WASP-style filtering corrects reference mapping bias. RNA editing : REDItools, SPRINT, JACUSA2. Small RNA : miRDeep2, sRNAbench, nf-core/smrnaseq; miRNA target prediction with TargetScan and miRDB. Deconvolution : estimate cell-type proportions from bulk with CIBERSORTx, MuSiC, BayesPrism, EPIC, quanTIseq, using single-cell references. Nascent and decay : PRO-seq, TT-seq and metabolic labelling (SLAM-seq) separate transcription from degradation. Tools Benchmarks Pitfalls Confounded design: if all cases were sequenced in batch 1 and all controls in batch 2, no software can separate batch from biology. Pseudoreplication: multiple wells from one donor are not independent biological replicates. Calling a pathway \"activated\" from an over-representation test that ignores direction and gene–gene correlation. Using a genome-wide background for enrichment when only expressed genes could have been detected. Treating \"not significant\" as \"not changed\"; with low power, absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. Research gap Accurate isoform-level quantification and differential testing, especially from long reads at modest depth. Calibrated DE in large heterogeneous cohorts, where standard parametric tools can inflate false positives. Separating transcriptional from post-transcriptional regulation in standard steady-state data. Robust deconvolution when reference single-cell data come from a different platform or disease state."}, {"id": "singlecell", "title": "Single-cell & spatial", "text": "RNA layer · resolution Single-cell and spatial omics Bulk profiles average thousands of cells. Single-cell methods measure each cell separately, revealing cell types, rare populations, continuous trajectories and cell-to-cell communication. Spatial methods add where each cell sits in the tissue. Research questions What cell types and states exist in a tissue, and which are new? How do cell composition and cell-type-specific expression change in disease? What are the developmental or differentiation trajectories, and what drives fate decisions? Which cells communicate, through which ligand–receptor pairs, and in which tissue niches? Which regulatory programmes (TFs, enhancers) define each state? How does a genetic variant or perturbation act in specific cell types (single-cell eQTLs, Perturb-seq)? Technologies Approach Examples Throughput Trade-off Droplet, 3′ or 5′ tag 10x Chromium (Flex, 3′, 5′), Drop-seq, inDrops 10 3 –10 5 cells per run Cheap per cell; sparse (~1–5k genes detected); no full-length isoforms Combinatorial indexing Parse Evercode, Scale Bio, sci-RNA-seq3 10 5 –10 6 + cells Fixed cells, many samples multiplexed; lower per-cell sensitivity Plate-based full-length Smart-seq2, Smart-seq3, FLASH-seq 10 2 –10 3 cells High sensitivity and isoform information; expensive per cell Single-nucleus snRNA-seq on frozen tissue as droplet Works on archived and hard-to-dissociate tissue (brain, muscle); more intronic reads, fewer transcripts Multi-modal CITE-seq (RNA + surface proteins), 10x Multiome (RNA + ATAC), TEA-seq, scNMT-seq, Perturb-seq, VDJ varies Two or more layers from the same cell: the strongest data for integration Sequencing-based spatial Visium (55 µm spots), Visium HD (2 µm bins), Slide-seqV2, Stereo-seq whole transcriptome Unbiased gene coverage; spots may mix several cells; need deconvolution or segmentation Imaging-based spatial Xenium, MERFISH/MERSCOPE, CosMx, seqFISH+, CODEX/PhenoCycler (protein), IMC 10 2 –10 3 + genes, subcellular Single-cell or subcellular resolution; targeted gene panels; segmentation errors matter How single-cell and spatial data are generated Tissue to cells Enzymatic + mechanical dissociation, or nuclei isolation from frozen tissue; keep cold and fast to limit stress genes Cell QC Count and viability (>80–90%); remove debris and dead cells; optional sorting or hashtag multiplexing Partition Microfluidics pairs each cell with one barcoded gel bead in an oil droplet (or barcodes are added in rounds of split-pool) Barcode in droplet Cell lyses; mRNA poly(A) tails bind bead oligos; reverse transcription writes cell barcode + UMI into each cDNA Pool & amplify Break the emulsion, amplify cDNA, build gene-expression (and VDJ, protein, ATAC) libraries Sequence ~20,000–50,000 reads per cell; Read 1 = barcode + UMI, Read 2 = transcript Spatial methods skip dissociation. In spot-based methods (Visium, Stereo-seq), a tissue section is placed on a slide printed with barcoded capture probes whose positions are known; RNA diffuses onto the probes, so each barcode doubles as a coordinate. In imaging-based methods (Xenium, MERFISH, CosMx), gene-specific probes are hybridised in the intact section and decoded by repeated rounds of fluorescence imaging, giving the position of each transcript. The scRNA-seq workflow Pre-processing : demultiplex cell barcodes and UMIs, align and count (Cell Ranger, STARsolo, alevin-fry, kallisto|bustools). Output: a sparse cells × genes count matrix (h5ad, h5, mtx). Cell QC : total UMIs, genes detected, mitochondrial fraction (dying cells), ribosomal fraction. Prefer data-driven thresholds (median absolute deviations) per sample over fixed cut-offs. Remove ambient RNA (SoupX, CellBender, DecontX) and doublets (scDblFinder, DoubletFinder, scrublet). Sample multiplexing by genotype (demuxlet, souporcell, vireo) or hashtags (HTO) also identifies doublets. Normalisation : size-factor scaling with log1p (shifted logarithm), scran pooling, sctransform or analytic Pearson residuals. Feature selection : 2,000–5,000 highly variable genes; deviance-based selection is an alternative. Dimensionality reduction : PCA (or a VAE latent space such as scVI), then a k-nearest-neighbour graph. UMAP and t-SNE are for visualisation only; distances and cluster sizes in them are not meaningful. Integration / batch correction across samples: Harmony, scVI/scANVI, Seurat CCA/RPCA, Scanorama, BBKNN, fastMNN, LIGER. Over-correction can erase real biology (e.g. disease-specific states). Clustering : Leiden or Louvain community detection on the kNN graph; check robustness across resolutions. Annotation : marker genes plus automated label transfer (CellTypist, SingleR, Azimuth, scANVI, scArches) to reference atlases (Human Cell Atlas, Tabula Sapiens, CELLxGENE Census). Downstream : compositional analysis (scCODA, propeller, milo for neighbourhoods), differential expression by pseudobulk per sample and cell type, trajectories, velocity, communication, gene-regulatory networks. The unit of replication is the donor, not the cell Ten thousand cells from two patients are two biological replicates. Single-cell DE tests that treat cells as independent produce floods of false positives. Aggregate counts per sample and cell type (pseudobulk) and use DESeq2, edgeR or limma, or use mixed models with a donor random effect. This is the most important statistical lesson of the field. Trajectories, velocity and fate Pseudotime orders cells along a continuous process (Monocle 3, Slingshot, PAGA, Palantir, diffusion pseudotime). RNA velocity uses the ratio of unspliced to spliced reads to estimate the direction of change (velocyto, scVelo, with newer models such as veloVI, UniTVelo and dynamo); its assumptions (constant rates, steady-state kinetics) often fail, so treat velocity arrows as hypotheses. CellRank combines velocity, pseudotime or real time points into fate probabilities. Lineage tracing with heritable barcodes (CRISPR scars, LARRY, mitochondrial variants) provides ground truth that computational trajectories cannot. Communication and regulation Ligand–receptor inference (CellPhoneDB, CellChat, NicheNet, LIANA+) scores co-expression of ligand in sender cells and receptor in receiver cells using curated databases. These are hypotheses: mRNA co-expression does not prove protein signalling, and results depend heavily on the resource used. Gene-regulatory network inference (SCENIC/pySCENIC: GRNBoost2 co-expression + cis-regulatory motif pruning; CellOracle; SCENIC+ with multiome data) identifies regulons and TF activity. Spatial analysis Segmentation of imaging data into cells: Cellpose, Baysor, proseg, StarDist; errors here propagate into every downstream result. Deconvolution of multi-cell spots using single-cell references: cell2location, RCTD (spacexr), CARD, Tangram, DestVI, SPOTlight. Spatially variable genes : SpatialDE, SPARK-X, nnSVG, Moran's I in Squidpy. Spatial domains / niches : BayesSpace, SpaGCN, GraphST, STAGATE, BANKSY, CellCharter. Neighbourhood and interaction : co-occurrence and neighbourhood enrichment (Squidpy), spatially constrained ligand–receptor scoring (COMMOT, LIANA+), niche-aware DE. Frameworks : Squidpy and SpatialData (Python, scverse), Giotto Suite, Seurat v5, Voyager, SpatialExperiment (Bioconductor). Foundation models Transformer models pre-trained on tens of millions of cells (Geneformer, scGPT, scFoundation, UCE, Nicheformer, and others) promise general-purpose embeddings for annotation, batch integration and perturbation prediction. Independent evaluations have been sobering: in zero-shot settings their embeddings often do not beat highly variable genes with PCA, scVI or Harmony, and for predicting perturbation effects deep models have not clearly outperformed simple linear or mean baselines. Fine-tuning helps for some tasks. Evaluate any claim against simple baselines on held-out data that the model did not see during pre-training. Tools Benchmarks Research gap Benchmarks and ground truth for spatial domain detection, cell segmentation and spatial communication. Statistical frameworks for comparing conditions across many donors in spatial data (multi-sample spatial DE). Honest evaluation and useful applications of single-cell foundation models; perturbation response prediction for unseen perturbations and cell types. Integrating many modalities measured on different cells (mosaic integration) without erasing biology. Defining \"cell state\" versus \"cell type\" in a reproducible, ontology-backed way."}, {"id": "proteomics", "title": "Proteomics", "text": "Protein layer Proteomics Proteins do most of the work in a cell and are the targets of most drugs. Proteomics identifies and quantifies them, their modifications and their interactions. There is no protein equivalent of PCR, so the field relies on mass spectrometry and affinity reagents, each with its own statistics. Research questions Which proteins and proteoforms differ between conditions, and do they follow mRNA changes? Which signalling pathways are active (phosphoproteomics) and which kinases drive them? Which plasma proteins are biomarkers or drug targets, and which are causal (pQTL + Mendelian randomisation)? Which proteins form complexes, where are they located, and how do structures explain variant effects? Which drug binds which protein (chemoproteomics, thermal proteome profiling)? How proteomic data are generated Sample Cells, tissue, plasma or serum (EDTA plasma preferred); avoid haemolysis and keratin contamination Lysis & quantify Detergent or urea buffers; protein amount by BCA assay Reduce & alkylate Break disulfide bonds (DTT/TCEP) and cap cysteines (iodoacetamide) Digest Trypsin overnight (or S-Trap/SP3 workflows); optional TMT labelling or PTM enrichment Clean up Desalt peptides (C18); measure peptide amount LC-MS/MS Nano- or micro-flow LC into an Orbitrap or timsTOF; DDA or DIA acquisition, 30–120 min per sample Affinity platforms differ: Olink incubates plasma with pairs of DNA-tagged antibodies (a signal only forms when both bind the same protein), and SomaScan uses modified aptamers. Both convert protein amounts into nucleic-acid or fluorescence readouts and need only microlitres of plasma. How bottom-up mass spectrometry works Sample preparation : lyse, reduce and alkylate cysteines, digest with trypsin (cuts after K/R) into peptides of ~7–30 residues. Optional enrichment for PTMs (phospho: TiO 2 , Fe-IMAC; ubiquitin: K-ε-GG antibody) or depletion of abundant plasma proteins. Liquid chromatography separates peptides by hydrophobicity over 10–120 minutes; they are ionised by electrospray. MS1 measures mass-to-charge (m/z) of intact peptide ions; MS2 fragments selected ions (CID/HCD, ETD) and measures fragment masses (b- and y-ions), which encode the sequence. Acquisition : DDA (data-dependent; picks the top-N most intense ions, giving stochastic missing values), DIA (data-independent; fragments all ions in m/z windows, more complete and reproducible, more complex spectra), targeted (SRM/PRM; predefined peptides, best for validation). Identification : match spectra against theoretical spectra from a protein database (MSFragger, Comet, Andromeda, Sage, Mascot) or spectral libraries (often predicted by deep learning: Prosit, AlphaPeptDeep, MS²PIP). Control FDR with target–decoy competition at peptide-spectrum match, peptide and protein levels (typically 1%); rescoring with Percolator or MS²Rescore. Protein inference : peptides shared between proteins create ambiguity, resolved into protein groups by parsimony. Quantification : label-free (MS1 intensity, MaxLFQ), isobaric tags (TMT/TMTpro: up to 18 samples multiplexed, measured at reporter ions; ratio compression from co-isolation), SILAC (metabolic labelling), or DIA fragment intensities. Top-down proteomics Measures intact proteins, so proteoforms with combinations of PTMs are preserved. Harder and lower throughput (ProSight, TopPIC). Affinity proteomics Olink (proximity extension assay; DNA-barcoded antibody pairs read by sequencing; ~5,400 proteins in Explore HT) and SomaScan (modified aptamers; ~11,000 assays). Scalable to biobanks (UK Biobank Pharma Proteomics Project). Platforms agree only moderately for many proteins, and epitope effects can create artefactual pQTLs. Single-cell and spatial SCoPE2, plexDIA and Astral-based single-cell proteomics quantify ~1,000–5,000 proteins per cell. Deep Visual Proteomics combines imaging, laser microdissection and MS. CODEX/PhenoCycler and IMC image ~40–100 proteins in tissue. Statistical analysis MS proteomics data have features that RNA-seq tools do not handle: Missing values that are not random: low-abundance peptides are missing because they fell below detection (MNAR), while others are missing by chance (MCAR). Imputation choice changes results (minimum-value, left-censored QRILC or MinProb for MNAR; kNN or random forest for MCAR). Models that handle missingness directly (MSstats, proDA, limpa) are preferable to blind imputation. Log-normal intensities : analyse log 2 intensities with linear models (limma, MSstats, MSstatsTMT, DEqMS which accounts for the number of peptides per protein). Normalisation : median centring, variance-stabilising normalisation (vsn), TMT reference channels (IRS) for multi-batch designs. PTM site localisation probabilities (ptmRS, PTMProphet), and normalising PTM changes to protein abundance. Kinase activity inference from phosphosites: KSEA, PTM-SEA, the Kinase Library (Johnson et al. 2023). Structure and interactions AlphaFold2 (2021) predicts single-chain structures with near-experimental accuracy for many proteins; the AlphaFold Protein Structure Database holds over 200 million predictions. AlphaFold3 (2024) and open alternatives (Boltz-1/2, Chai-1, RoseTTAFold All-Atom) predict complexes with nucleic acids, ligands and modifications. ESMFold predicts structure from a protein language model without multiple-sequence alignments. pLDDT and PAE confidence scores must be read before trusting a model: low pLDDT often marks intrinsically disordered regions. Interaction resources include STRING (integrated scores), BioGRID and IntAct (curated), BioPlex and HuRI (systematic AP-MS and Y2H maps), and CORUM (complexes). Tools Benchmarks Pitfall Protein FDR is not peptide FDR. With large datasets, a 1% peptide-level FDR can produce a much higher protein-level FDR if not controlled separately. Report which levels were controlled. Also beware of \"match between runs\", which improves completeness in DDA but can transfer wrong identifications if not FDR-controlled. Research gap Proteoform-resolved quantification: linking specific splice isoforms and PTM combinations to function. Principled missing-value modelling and uncertainty propagation from spectra to proteins. Single-cell proteomics statistics and integration with scRNA-seq. Cross-platform harmonisation of Olink, SomaScan and MS, and resolving epitope-driven artefacts in pQTLs. Predicting dynamics and conformational ensembles, not single static structures; variant effects on complexes."}, {"id": "metabolomics", "title": "Metabolomics", "text": "Small-molecule layer Metabolomics Metabolites are the substrates, products and signals of biochemistry: sugars, amino acids, nucleotides, lipids, cofactors, drugs and microbial products. They change within seconds and integrate genes, environment, diet and microbiome, which makes them the omics layer closest to phenotype. Research questions Which metabolites or pathways distinguish disease, and can they be biomarkers? How do genes (mQTLs), diet, drugs and gut microbes shape circulating metabolites? Where does carbon flow through central metabolism in a tumour or immune cell (isotope tracing)? What is this unknown feature? (structure elucidation is the bottleneck of the field) What has a person been exposed to (exposome)? Technologies Platform Strengths Weaknesses Typical use LC-MS (reversed phase, HILIC) Broad coverage, sensitive, MS/MS for identification Ion suppression, batch drift, many features per metabolite (adducts, isotopes, in-source fragments) Untargeted discovery, lipidomics GC-MS Reproducible retention, large EI spectral libraries (NIST) Needs derivatisation and volatility Central carbon metabolism, organic acids NMR Quantitative, non-destructive, highly reproducible, structural information ~100× less sensitive than MS Large cohorts (e.g. Nightingale lipoprotein panels), flux Targeted triple-quad MRM Absolute quantification with standards Only predefined metabolites Validation, clinical assays (Biocrates kits) MS imaging (MALDI, DESI) Spatial distribution in tissue Identification and quantification hard Spatial metabolomics How metabolomic data are generated Collect & quench Stop metabolism instantly: cold methanol, liquid nitrogen; process plasma within ~30 min on ice Extract Methanol/acetonitrile for polar metabolites; MTBE or Folch/Bligh–Dyer for lipids; add internal standards Prepare Dry and reconstitute; derivatise for GC-MS; make a pooled QC from all samples Run order Randomise samples; inject blanks and pooled QCs every 5–10 samples; MS/MS on pooled QCs for identification LC-MS / GC-MS / NMR Reversed phase (lipids, non-polar) and HILIC (polar), positive and negative ionisation Raw output Vendor files → mzML; NMR free-induction decays → spectra Untargeted LC-MS workflow Design : randomised injection order, pooled QC samples every 5–10 injections, blanks, internal standards, and long-term reference material (NIST SRM 1950 plasma). Conversion : vendor files to open formats (mzML) with ProteoWizard msconvert. Feature detection : find chromatographic peaks in m/z–retention-time space (XCMS centWave, MZmine 3 ADAP, MS-DIAL, OpenMS FeatureFinderMetabo). Alignment and grouping across samples (retention-time correction), gap filling. Annotation of feature redundancy : group adducts ([M+H] + , [M+Na] + ), isotopes and fragments of the same compound (CAMERA, MS-FLO, IPA). Drift and batch correction : QC-based LOESS or random forest (QC-RLSC, SERRF), and filter features with high CV in QCs (>20–30%). Identification : match accurate mass, retention time and MS/MS spectra to libraries (MoNA, MassBank, GNPS, NIST, mzCloud) or predict structures (SIRIUS with CSI:FingerID, CANOPUS compound classes, MS-FINDER, MetFrag). Molecular networking (GNPS/FBMN) clusters related spectra so annotations propagate. Statistics : log or generalised-log transform, scaling (Pareto, auto), univariate tests with FDR, multivariate PCA, PLS-DA and OPLS-DA (with permutation testing and cross-validation; PLS-DA overfits easily). Pathway analysis : over-representation on KEGG, SMPDB or Reactome (MetaboAnalyst), or annotation-free mummichog / PIUMet that work directly from m/z values. Identification confidence (Metabolomics Standards Initiative) Level 1 : confirmed with an authentic standard (same RT and MS/MS on the same system). Level 2 : probable structure from library spectrum match. Level 3 : tentative compound class. Level 4 : unknown. Schymanski et al. (2014) added finer levels for high-resolution MS. Always report the level. Most untargeted features remain at level 3–4. Flux and metabolic models Lipids are measured with the same instruments but have their own extraction, standards, nomenclature and quantification rules; they are covered in the Lipidomics chapter , with a focus on targeted methods. Stable-isotope tracing follows 13 C-glucose or 13 C-glutamine into downstream metabolites; isotopologue distributions are corrected for natural abundance (IsoCor, AccuCor) and fluxes estimated with INCA or 13CFLUX2. Genome-scale metabolic models (Recon3D, Human1/Human-GEM, AGORA2 for gut microbes) predict fluxes by flux balance analysis under constraints (COBRApy, COBRA Toolbox, RAVEN); omics data constrain them to tissue or patient-specific models (GIMME, iMAT, tINIT). Tools Benchmarks Research gap The \"dark metabolome\": most MS/MS spectra in untargeted studies are unannotated. Structure prediction, in silico libraries and ML-based class annotation are active areas. Cross-lab reproducibility and harmonisation of feature tables. Absolute quantification at scale and combining metabolomics with fluxes. Distinguishing host-, microbe- and diet-derived metabolites in integrated host–microbiome models. Spatial metabolomics at single-cell resolution."}, {"id": "lipidomics", "title": "Lipidomics (targeted)", "text": "Small-molecule layer · lipids Lipidomics, with a focus on targeted methods Lipids build membranes, store energy and carry signals. Human plasma contains thousands of lipid species from a few dozen classes. Targeted lipidomics measures a predefined panel of these species with internal standards, giving reproducible concentrations that can be compared across cohorts and laboratories. Research questions Which lipid species change in disease, and can a small panel predict risk (e.g. ceramides for cardiovascular events)? How do genes (lipid QTLs), diet, drugs such as statins, and the microbiome shape the plasma lipidome? Which enzymes and pathways (sphingolipid synthesis, fatty-acid elongation and desaturation, phospholipid remodelling) are altered in a tissue or cell? How do signalling lipids (oxylipins, lysophospholipids, sphingosine-1-phosphate, endocannabinoids) relate to inflammation? Where are lipids located in a tissue (MALDI imaging), and how does membrane composition change with cell state? The lipid classes you will meet Category (LIPID MAPS) Main classes Roles Typical ion and fragment Glycerophospholipids PC, PE, PS, PI, PG, PA, and lyso forms (LPC, LPE) Membrane bilayer; PI phosphates in signalling; LPC as a signal and damage marker PC/LPC: [M+H]⁺ → m/z 184.07 (phosphocholine); PE: neutral loss of 141; PS: neutral loss of 185 Sphingolipids Ceramides (Cer), sphingomyelin (SM), hexosylceramides, gangliosides, S1P Membrane rafts, apoptosis, insulin resistance, cardiovascular risk Cer: [M+H]⁺ → m/z 264.27 (d18:1 sphingoid base); SM: m/z 184.07 Glycerolipids Triacylglycerols (TG), diacylglycerols (DG) Energy storage, lipotoxicity, DG as a signal [M+NH₄]⁺ → neutral loss of one fatty acid (+NH₃) Sterol lipids Cholesterol, cholesteryl esters (CE), bile acids, steroid hormones Membrane rigidity, lipoprotein transport, hormones CE: [M+NH₄]⁺ → m/z 369.35 (cholestadiene) Fatty acyls Free fatty acids, acylcarnitines, oxylipins (prostaglandins, leukotrienes, HETEs), endocannabinoids Fuel, inflammation and resolution signals Negative mode [M−H]⁻; oxylipins need dedicated targeted panels Names follow the LIPID MAPS shorthand, and the name should only claim what the data show ( Liebisch et al. 2020, Journal of Lipid Research ): PC 34:1 species level: class + total carbons:double bonds (from MS1 or a class fragment) PC 16:0_18:1 molecular species level: the two fatty acyls are known, their positions are not (needs acyl fragments) PC 16:0/18:1 sn-position level: palmitate at sn-1, oleate at sn-2 PC 16:0/18:1(9Z) full structure: double-bond position and geometry (needs OzID, EAD/UVPD or chemical derivatisation) A standard MRM transition to m/z 184 tells you the lipid is a phosphocholine-containing species of a given mass, so most targeted panels report PC and SM at the species level unless acyl-specific transitions are added. Three strategies Untargeted LC-HRMS Shotgun (direct infusion) Targeted LC-MRM Instrument Orbitrap or Q-TOF with data-dependent MS/MS High-resolution MS or triple quad, no chromatography Triple quadrupole (QqQ), sometimes QTRAP Coverage Broad, including unexpected lipids Hundreds to ~1,000+ species, fast Predefined panel, typically 100–1,000+ transitions Quantification Relative (fold change), semi-quantitative with standards Quantitative with class standards Most accurate and reproducible; absolute with calibration Isomers Partly separated by RT Not separated; ion suppression in the mixture Separated if chromatography and transitions are designed for it Best for Discovery High-throughput profiling Validation, large cohorts, clinical assays A common design is to discover candidates with untargeted lipidomics in a small cohort, then build a targeted MRM assay for those lipids (plus their class standards) and measure it in a large, independent cohort. How targeted lipidomics data are generated Collect EDTA plasma or serum on ice, separated within ~1 h; lysophospholipids and oxylipins keep forming ex vivo at room temperature Spike standards Add class-specific internal standards (deuterated or odd-chain, e.g. SPLASH LIPIDOMIX) before extraction, so losses are corrected Extract MTBE (Matyash), chloroform/methanol (Folch, Bligh–Dyer) or butanol/methanol (BUME); add antioxidant (BHT) for oxidisable lipids; SPE for oxylipins Separate HILIC groups each class with its standard; reversed phase separates species by chain length and unsaturation Scheduled MRM QqQ monitors each precursor → product transition only in its retention-time window, allowing hundreds of lipids per run QC design Pooled QC every 5–10 samples, blanks, calibration standards, and a reference plasma (NIST SRM 1950) in every batch From peak areas to concentrations Integrate peaks for each transition (Skyline, MultiQuant/SCIEX OS, TargetLynx, TraceFinder, MRMkit). Check peak boundaries and retention times; automated integration errors are the most common silent problem. Normalise to the internal standard : response ratio = analyte area / internal-standard area. Isotope correction : type I corrects a species' own signal for its natural ¹³C distribution; type II removes overlap from the M+2 isotope of the species with one more double bond, which falls on the same nominal mass. Quantify , at one of three levels of rigour: Single-point, class-based : concentration = response ratio × known IS concentration. Assumes every species in a class responds like the standard, which is approximately true in HILIC and shotgun but not for very different chain lengths. Response-factor corrected : apply measured correction factors for chain length and unsaturation. Calibration curve : authentic standards at several concentrations, ideally with a stable-isotope-labelled version of each analyte. This is the only route to absolute concentrations that hold across laboratories and is required for clinical assays. Assay performance : linearity and range, limit of detection (LOD, often signal-to-noise ≥ 3) and limit of quantification (LOQ, S/N ≥ 10 or CV ≤ 20%), precision (intra- and inter-batch CV), accuracy (recovery of spiked standards), carry-over, and matrix effects. Batch and drift correction with pooled QCs (LOESS, SERRF, QC-RLSC); remove species with QC CV above ~20–30% or below LOQ in most samples. Report in absolute units (nmol/mL plasma, pmol/µg protein, pmol/10⁶ cells), with the annotation level and the standards used. Pitfalls Isobaric overlap: different lipids with the same nominal mass and fragment (e.g. PC and some PE species, or ether vs ester lipids) are counted together unless separated by retention time or resolved by specific fragments. In-source fragmentation can create false signals for lyso lipids and ceramides. Using one internal standard for many classes, or adding it after extraction, cannot correct class-specific recovery. \"Absolute\" concentrations from single-point class quantification are approximations; say so in the methods. Relative units (percentage of total lipid) are compositional and behave like microbiome data. Statistics and interpretation Transform and test : log-transform concentrations; linear models with covariates (age, sex, BMI, fasting, statin use) and FDR control. Lipids are highly correlated, so also consider class-level sums, ratios (e.g. Cer/SM) and regularised models. Enrichment : lipid ontology enrichment with LION/web, or class- and chain-property enrichment (Lipid Mini-On, lipidr). Plot effects by class against chain length and number of double bonds to reveal patterns such as \"saturated TGs up, polyunsaturated down\". Pathways : map species to reactions (LIPID MAPS, Reactome, BioPAN for pathway activity from lipid ratios) and connect them to enzyme genes in transcriptomic or genetic data. Genetics : lipid QTL studies link variants in genes such as FADS1/2 (fatty-acid desaturation) and SPTLC (ceramide synthesis) to specific species, and support Mendelian randomisation of lipids on disease. Example: ceramides as a clinical test Plasma ratios of specific ceramides, such as Cer(d18:1/16:0), Cer(d18:1/18:0) and Cer(d18:1/24:1) relative to Cer(d18:1/24:0), predicted cardiovascular death in patients with coronary artery disease beyond LDL cholesterol ( Laaksonen et al. 2016, European Heart Journal ). These measurements are now offered as clinical risk scores. They work clinically because they use targeted LC-MRM with authentic, stable-isotope-labelled standards and calibration curves, which make concentrations comparable across laboratories. Tools Benchmarks and standardisation Research gap Isomer-resolved quantification (sn-position, double-bond position and geometry) at cohort scale. Absolute quantification without an authentic standard for every species: better response-factor models and predicted ionisation efficiencies. Harmonising values across labs and platforms, including untargeted, shotgun and targeted data. Linking lipid species to enzymes and genes for causal inference (lipid QTLs, Mendelian randomisation, isotope tracing of lipid synthesis). Spatial and single-cell lipidomics (MALDI-2 imaging, mass cytometry of lipids) and their quantification. Machine-learning annotation of lipid MS/MS spectra and automated QC of MRM peak integration."}, {"id": "microbiome", "title": "Metagenomics & microbiome", "text": "Community layer Metagenomics and the microbiome Humans carry roughly as many bacterial cells as human cells, and the gut microbiome encodes far more genes than our own genome. Metagenomics sequences the whole community to ask who is there, what they can do, and how they interact with the host. Research questions How does community composition differ in disease, and is it cause or consequence? Which functions (pathways, antibiotic resistance genes, virulence factors) are present and expressed? Which strains are shared between mother and infant, or transmitted between people? Which microbial metabolites (short-chain fatty acids, bile acids, TMAO) mediate effects on the host? Which genomes have never been cultured (metagenome-assembled genomes)? How microbiome data are generated Collect Stool, swab, saliva, soil, water; stabilising buffer or immediate freezing; record diet, drugs, antibiotics Extract DNA Bead-beating to break tough Gram-positive walls; include extraction blanks and a mock community Amplicon or shotgun 16S/ITS PCR with barcoded primers, or fragment all DNA for shotgun libraries Sequence Amplicons: 2 × 250–300 bp, ~20–50k reads/sample; shotgun: 5–20 M reads/sample, long reads for genomes Controls Negative controls to detect contamination; positive mock communities to measure bias Approaches Amplicon (16S rRNA, ITS, 18S) PCR of a marker gene's variable regions (e.g. V4). Cheap and sensitive; genus-level (sometimes species) taxonomy; no direct function. Denoise into exact amplicon sequence variants (ASVs) with DADA2, Deblur or UNOISE rather than 97% OTUs. Classify with SILVA, GTDB or Greengenes2. Infer function with PICRUSt2 (predicted, so interpret with care). Shotgun metagenomics Sequences all DNA. Taxonomic profiling by marker genes (MetaPhlAn 4) or k-mers (Kraken 2 + Bracken, sylph); functional profiling (HUMAnN 3); assembly (MEGAHIT, metaSPAdes, metaFlye), binning into MAGs (MetaBAT 2, SemiBin2, VAMB, DAS Tool), quality with CheckM2 (MIMAG completeness and contamination standards), taxonomy with GTDB-Tk; strain tracking (StrainPhlAn, inStrain). Meta-transcriptomics, -proteomics, -metabolomics Which genes are expressed (HUMAnN on RNA), which proteins are made, and which metabolites are present. Integrating these with the metagenome separates potential function from realised activity. The statistics of compositions Microbiome counts are compositional (only relative abundances are observed), sparse (many zeros), and overdispersed . Rarefying throws away data; total-sum scaling ignores compositionality. Use compositionally aware methods: centred or additive log-ratio transforms (CLR, ALR), ANCOM-BC2, ALDEx2, LinDA, or regression frameworks such as MaAsLin 2/3 with careful normalisation. Diversity: alpha (richness, Shannon, Faith's PD) and beta (Bray–Curtis, UniFrac, Aitchison distance) tested with PERMANOVA (vegan adonis2), checking dispersion first (betadisper). Pitfalls Contamination dominates low-biomass samples (tissue, blood, placenta). Include extraction and PCR blanks and use decontam. Host DNA can be over 90% of reads in tissue; deplete or sequence deeper. Medication (metformin, proton-pump inhibitors, antibiotics), diet and transit time confound case–control differences. Different differential-abundance methods give different answers on the same data; report results from several methods or a consensus. Tools Benchmarks Research gap Moving from association to mechanism: causal inference, gnotobiotic validation, metabolite mediation. Strain-level functional differences and horizontal gene transfer tracking. Viromes and phages, the least annotated part of the microbiome. Absolute abundance (flow cytometry, spike-ins, qPCR) instead of relative abundance. Integrating microbial genomes, host genotype and metabolome in one causal model."}, {"id": "more", "title": "More omics", "text": "Specialised layers More omics worth knowing Specialised layers that sit between the main ones. Each answers a question the big four cannot, and several are fertile ground for methods development because their tooling is younger. Epitranscriptomics More than 170 RNA modifications are known. m6A (N6-methyladenosine), written by METTL3/METTL14, read by YTHDF/YTHDC proteins and erased by FTO/ALKBH5, affects mRNA stability, splicing and translation. Mapping: antibody-based MeRIP-seq/m6A-seq (peaks, ~100–200 nt resolution) and miCLIP (single-nucleotide), antibody-free GLORI and eTAM-seq (quantitative stoichiometry), and direct RNA nanopore sequencing , where modifications alter the current signal (m6Anet, CHEUI, xPore, Dorado modification models). Pseudouridine (BID-seq), m5C (bisulfite on RNA) and A-to-I editing are the other common targets. Gap: quantitative, isoform-resolved maps and validation of nanopore-based calls. Translatomics Ribo-seq sequences ribosome-protected fragments (~28–30 nt) and shows where ribosomes are, with 3-nt periodicity revealing the reading frame. Uses: translation efficiency (Ribo-seq / RNA-seq; anota2seq, Xtail, riborex, deltaTE), discovery of upstream and small ORFs and micropeptides (RiboCode, ORFquant, PRICE, RiboTISH, Ribo-TISH), and ribosome stalling. QC with RiboseQC and riboWaltz. GENCODE now catalogues Ribo-seq ORFs (Mudge et al. 2022). Immunomics and immune repertoires T- and B-cell receptors are generated by V(D)J recombination, producing ~10 15 possible receptors. AIRR-seq sequences these from bulk or single cells (10x VDJ). Tools: MiXCR, IgBLAST, TRUST4 (from RNA-seq), Immcantation (change-O, SHazaM for somatic hypermutation, clonal lineages), scirpy (single-cell, scverse). Questions: clonal expansion, convergent responses to vaccines and infection, antigen specificity prediction (TCRdist, GLIPH2, and deep models with modest generalisation to unseen epitopes). Immunopeptidomics measures HLA-presented peptides by MS to train neoantigen predictors (NetMHCpan, MHCflurry). Glycomics and glycoproteomics Glycans are branched, non-templated structures attached to proteins and lipids. They control protein folding, cell recognition, immune function (IgG Fc glycosylation tunes antibody effector activity) and are altered in cancer. Analysis: released-glycan MS or HPLC, intact glycopeptide MS (pGlyco, MSFragger-Glyco, Byonic), databases GlyTouCan and GlyGen. Gap: structural isomers are hard to distinguish, and glycan biosynthesis is only indirectly coded in the genome. Cell-free DNA, liquid biopsy and fragmentomics Dying cells release DNA fragments into blood, with a ~167 bp peak reflecting nucleosome protection. Tumour-derived cfDNA (ctDNA) can be detected through mutations (deep targeted sequencing with UMIs), copy number (low-pass WGS; ichorCNA estimates tumour fraction), methylation patterns that also reveal tissue of origin (multi-cancer early detection tests), and fragment size, end motifs and coverage at TSSs (DELFI and related fragmentomics methods). Non-invasive prenatal testing uses the same principles. Gap: sensitivity for early-stage cancers, where ctDNA fractions can be below 0.1%. Interactomics and functional genomics screens Pooled CRISPR screens (knockout, CRISPRi, CRISPRa, base and prime editing) measure fitness or reporter effects for every gene or variant; analyse with MAGeCK, BAGEL2, CRISPResso2 (editing outcomes) and the DepMap resources (Chronos-corrected gene effects across ~1,000 cancer cell lines). Perturb-seq combines CRISPR perturbations with single-cell RNA readouts; genome-scale Perturb-seq (Replogle et al. 2022) maps transcriptional consequences of nearly every expressed gene. MPRAs (massively parallel reporter assays) test thousands of regulatory variants for activity at once. Phenomics, imaging and clinical omics Cell Painting : multiplexed fluorescent stains and CellProfiler or deep features produce morphological profiles for drug and gene perturbations (JUMP-Cell Painting consortium). Pathomics : deep learning on whole-slide H&E images predicts mutations, expression and survival; foundation models (UNI, CONCH, Virchow, Prov-GigaPath) provide general-purpose embeddings. Radiogenomics : linking imaging features to molecular subtypes. EHR phenomics : phenotype definitions (phecodes, HPO, OMOP common data model) enable PheWAS and biobank-scale genetics. Exposomics The exposome is the totality of environmental exposures over a lifetime: chemicals, diet, pollutants, infections, social factors. High-resolution MS of blood and urine detects thousands of exogenous chemicals; ExWAS (exposome-wide association studies) parallel GWAS. Gaps: chemical identification (even darker than the endogenous metabolome), time-varying exposure, and correlated exposures that confound each other."}, {"id": "relations", "title": "How the layers talk", "text": "Multi-omics · biology How the layers talk to each other Before integrating data computationally, decide what biological relationship you expect between the layers. The relationship determines which features you can link, which direction effects flow, how strong correlations should be, and which method is appropriate. Six kinds of relationship Relationship Mechanism How to link features Expected signal Genome → every layer (cis) A variant changes its own gene's regulation, splicing, coding sequence or structure Genomic position: variant within ±1 Mb of the gene (cis window) QTLs: strong, replicable, often small effect on traits Genome → every layer (trans) A variant in a TF, enzyme or receptor affects distant genes or metabolites Genome-wide scan; network propagation Weaker, harder to detect; large sample sizes needed Epigenome → transcriptome Accessibility and marks at promoters and enhancers control transcription Peak-to-gene links: distance, Hi-C contacts, correlation across samples or cells Promoter accessibility correlates positively, promoter CpG-island methylation negatively, with expression Transcriptome → proteome mRNA is template for protein; translation and decay modulate the result Gene ↔ protein ID mapping (Ensembl, UniProt); isoform ↔ proteoform Moderate positive correlation; time-lagged; buffered for complex subunits Proteome → metabolome Enzymes convert substrates; transporters move them Reaction networks (KEGG, Reactome, Recon3D/Human-GEM): enzyme ↔ substrate/product Weak and often non-monotonic; flux depends on substrate, cofactors and allostery, not only enzyme level Metabolome → epigenome, proteome Metabolites as cofactors (SAM, acetyl-CoA, α-KG, NAD + ) and allosteric regulators Prior knowledge of cofactor-dependent enzymes Feedback loops; hard to see without perturbation or time series Why layers disagree A frequent surprise is that layers measured on the same samples correlate less than expected. That disagreement is usually biology, not a failed experiment: Different timescales. Metabolites turn over in seconds, mRNAs in minutes to hours, proteins in hours to weeks. After a stimulus, mRNA peaks and falls before protein rises. Snapshot data at one time point capture different phases. Post-transcriptional regulation. Translation efficiency, microRNAs, RNA-binding proteins and protein degradation (ubiquitin–proteasome, autophagy) all intervene between mRNA and protein. Buffering and dosage compensation. Subunits of complexes are degraded when produced in excess, so an extra chromosome copy (aneuploidy) raises mRNA more than protein for complex members. Enzyme kinetics. Flux depends on substrate concentration relative to K m , on allosteric control and on cofactors. A doubled enzyme often does not double flux. Compartments and cell types. Bulk tissue mixes cells; a metabolite may come from a different cell type, or from microbes, than the enzyme you measured. Technical noise and detection limits. Each platform measures a different subset with different dynamic range and missingness. Evidence Reviews of mRNA–protein relationships conclude that mRNA levels explain a substantial but incomplete part of protein variation across genes, and much less across samples for the same gene, with translation rates and protein turnover accounting for much of the rest ( Liu, Beyer & Aebersold 2016, Cell ; Buccitelli & Selbach 2020, Nature Reviews Genetics ). In tumour cohorts (CPTAC), mRNA–protein correlation per gene across samples has a median around 0.4–0.5, lowest for stable housekeeping and complex proteins. The QTL framework: genetics as the anchor Genotype is fixed at conception and is not changed by disease, so associations from genotype to a molecular trait have a known causal direction. This makes molecular QTL mapping the backbone of most multi-omics interpretation: variant rs… accessibility · methylation expression splicing protein level metabolite disease / trait caQTL / meQTL eQTL sQTL pQTL mediation GWAS metabolite QTL (mQTL) Fig. 14.1 · Molecular QTLs connect one variant to each layer. If the same causal variant drives a molecular QTL and a GWAS signal, that molecule is a candidate mediator of the trait. Mapping : linear regression of normalised molecular phenotype on genotype dosage with covariates (genotype PCs, hidden factors from PEER or PCA). Tools: tensorQTL (GPU), QTLtools, FastQTL, MatrixEQTL; single-cell: pseudobulk per cell type or SAIGE-QTL, jaxQTL. Resources : GTEx (49 tissues, eQTL and sQTL), eQTLGen (blood eQTLs, ~31,000 samples), the eQTL Catalogue (uniformly processed studies), OneK1K (single-cell blood eQTLs), UK Biobank PPP and deCODE (pQTLs), and the GoDMC (methylation QTLs). From association to causation Colocalisation Do the GWAS and QTL signals share one causal variant, or are they two distinct variants in LD? coloc (Bayesian; posterior H4 for shared variant), coloc-SuSiE for multiple signals, eCAVIAR, HyPrColoc for many traits at once. TWAS / PWAS Predict gene expression (or protein) from genotype in a reference panel, then associate predicted expression with the trait in GWAS summary statistics (PrediXcan/S-PrediXcan, FUSION, UTMOST). Fast, but hits can reflect LD and co-regulation, not causal genes. Mendelian randomisation Use variants as instruments for an exposure (e.g. a cis-pQTL for a protein) to estimate its causal effect on an outcome. Assumes relevance, independence and no horizontal pleiotropy. Tools: TwoSampleMR, MendelianRandomization, MR-PRESSO, SMR with HEIDI test. Cis-pQTL MR is now used to prioritise drug targets. Example: PCSK9 across the layers Rare loss-of-function variants in PCSK9 lower LDL cholesterol and coronary disease risk (genome → phenome). PCSK9 protein binds the LDL receptor and targets it for degradation (proteome), so less PCSK9 means more receptors and lower plasma LDL (metabolome/lipidome). The genetics predicted that inhibiting PCSK9 would be safe and effective, and antibody (evolocumab, alirocumab) and siRNA (inclisiran) drugs followed. Drug programmes with human genetic support are roughly two to three times more likely to reach approval ( Minikel et al. 2024, Nature ). Linking features between layers in practice From → to Key Resources Watch out for Variant → gene Coordinates + regulatory links VEP, Open Targets Locus-to-Gene (L2G), ABC/rE2G predictions, cS2G Nearest gene is often wrong for non-coding variants Peak → gene Distance, contacts, correlation GREAT, ChIPseeker, ArchR/Signac peak-to-gene links One peak can regulate several genes and vice versa CpG → gene Probe annotation Illumina manifests, IlluminaHumanMethylationEPICv2anno Gene-body vs promoter methylation have opposite associations Gene → transcript → protein Ensembl gene/transcript/protein IDs, UniProt accession biomaRt, org.Hs.eg.db, UniProt ID mapping, MyGene.info Many-to-many mappings; protein groups contain several genes Protein → metabolite EC numbers, reaction IDs KEGG, Reactome, Rhea, Recon3D, Human-GEM, MetaNetX Promiscuous enzymes; metabolites in many reactions (ATP, NADH) Metabolite IDs InChIKey, HMDB, ChEBI, KEGG, PubChem CID RefMet, MetaboAnalyst ID converter, BridgeDb Isomers and lipid annotation levels map ambiguously Taxon → function → metabolite Gene families (UniRef90, KEGG KO) HUMAnN, AGORA2, gutMGene, MiMeNet Genetic potential is not activity Research gap Many GWAS loci do not colocalise with any known eQTL (\"missing regulation\"); context-specific, single-cell and developmental QTLs may close the gap ( Mostafavi et al. 2023, Nature Genetics ). Time-resolved multi-omics that captures lags between layers. Causal models across more than two layers (variant → methylation → expression → protein → trait) with proper uncertainty. Protein-to-metabolite links that account for flux, not just enzyme abundance."}, {"id": "integration", "title": "Integrating multi-omics data", "text": "Multi-omics · methods Integrating multi-omics data Integration means analysing two or more layers jointly so that the result is more than the sum of separate analyses. The right method depends on the question, on whether layers come from the same samples or cells, and on how much prior knowledge you want to impose. First, define the integration task Goal Question Method families Subtyping Are there patient groups visible across layers? Multi-view clustering (SNF, iClusterBayes, intNMF, NEMO), latent factors + clustering Factor discovery What shared and layer-specific axes of variation exist? MOFA+, JIVE, MCIA, RGCCA, GFA Prediction Which combination of features predicts outcome? DIABLO (sparse PLS-DA), stacked/late-fusion models, multi-modal deep learning, penalised Cox models Mechanism Which molecular chain links genotype to phenotype? QTL + colocalisation + MR, mediation analysis, causal networks Network / pathway Which pathways are dysregulated across layers? Multi-layer networks, prize-collecting Steiner trees (Omics Integrator), PARADIGM, COSMOS, multi-omics GSEA (mitch, ActivePathways, multiGSEA) Cell-level alignment Match cells across modalities WNN (Seurat v4/5), totalVI, MultiVI, GLUE, MOFA+, Cobolt, StabMap, MIDAS When to combine: early, intermediate, late Early (concatenation) Intermediate (joint latent space) Late (combine results) one model Z factors, clusters vote / stack + simple, captures cross-layer interactions − dominated by the largest or noisiest layer − needs aggressive feature selection and scaling + models shared and specific variation + handles different noise models per layer − factors need biological interpretation + robust; each layer modelled natively + tolerates missing layers per sample − misses interactions between layers Fig. 15.1 · The three integration strategies. Most current methods are intermediate: they learn a latent representation Z shared across layers. Sample structure determines what is possible Matched (vertical) All layers measured on the same samples or cells. Allows true joint models (MOFA+, DIABLO, WNN, totalVI). The anchor is the sample. Unmatched (diagonal) Different samples or cells per layer, same biology. Needs feature-level links (gene activity from ATAC, prior graphs): GLUE, LIGER/UINMF, Seurat bridge integration. Riskier assumptions. Mosaic Some samples have some layers. Methods: MultiVI, StabMap, MIDAS, Cobolt. Common in real consortia data. Horizontal Same layer across batches or studies. This is batch integration (Harmony, scVI), not multi-omics, but usually comes first. Core method families explained Matrix factorisation and latent factor models Each layer X m (samples × features) is approximated as Z·W m T , where Z (samples × K factors) is shared and W m holds layer-specific weights. MOFA/MOFA+ is a Bayesian group factor analysis with sparsity priors and per-layer likelihoods (Gaussian, Bernoulli, Poisson); it reports variance explained per factor per layer, so you see which factors are shared and which are private. JIVE separates joint from individual structure explicitly. MCIA and RGCCA maximise covariance between layer-specific projections. intNMF uses non-negative factorisation suited to clustering. iCluster/iClusterBayes fit a joint latent variable model for subtype discovery. Similarity and network fusion SNF builds a patient-similarity network per layer and iteratively fuses them through message passing until they converge; spectral clustering of the fused network gives subtypes. Robust to different scales and noise, easy to apply, but feature-level interpretation is indirect. NEMO extends this to partial data. Supervised multi-block methods DIABLO (mixOmics) finds sparse components that are correlated across layers and discriminate a categorical outcome, controlled by a design matrix of how strongly layers should correlate. Good for biomarker panels with cross-validation. Multi-block sPLS and penalised regression with layer-specific penalties (priority-Lasso, IPF-Lasso) are alternatives. Deep generative models Variational autoencoders with modality-specific encoders and decoders learn a joint latent space while modelling count noise properly: totalVI (RNA + surface proteins), MultiVI (RNA + ATAC, with mosaic support), PeakVI , GLUE (uses a prior regulatory graph to link unpaired modalities), scMM , MIDAS . Bulk-level examples include multi-omics autoencoders for survival (e.g. in TCGA). Graph neural networks operate on biological networks with omics features as node attributes. These models are flexible but need more data and careful evaluation against simple baselines. Knowledge-driven integration Instead of letting the data discover structure, map all layers onto prior networks: pathways (Reactome, KEGG), signalling (OmniPath), metabolic models (Human-GEM), regulatory networks (CollecTRI). COSMOS links phosphoproteomics, transcriptomics and metabolomics through a causal prior knowledge network using integer linear programming. Omics Integrator solves a prize-collecting Steiner forest to connect measured hits through the interactome. ActivePathways merges p-values across layers per pathway. These are more interpretable but limited by the completeness and bias of the prior knowledge. A practical recipe Write down the question and the relationship you expect (previous chapter). Pick the method family from the task table. Process each layer well on its own first : QC, normalisation, batch correction, and single-layer results. If a layer is uninformative alone, integration rarely rescues it. Harmonise samples and identifiers : one sample sheet with IDs per layer; check sample identity across layers (genotype concordance from RNA and DNA, sex markers) to detect swaps. Transform to comparable scales : variance-stabilised counts, log intensities, M-values, CLR for compositions. Scale features or weight layers so the largest layer does not dominate. Select features (highly variable per layer) to keep layers balanced and reduce noise. Handle missing data explicitly: methods that tolerate missing views (MOFA+, MultiVI, late fusion) are better than imputing whole layers. Fit, then inspect : variance explained per layer, factor–covariate associations (batch must not drive factors), stability across random seeds and subsamples. Interpret with feature weights, enrichment per factor, and links back to known biology. Validate on an independent cohort or with an orthogonal experiment. For prediction, nest feature selection inside cross-validation to avoid leakage. Tools Benchmarks The honest summary of the benchmarks No integration method wins everywhere. Adding layers does not automatically improve subtyping or prediction; a well-processed single layer (often transcriptomics) can match multi-omics models. Gains are largest when layers carry complementary information and sample sizes are adequate. Simple baselines (concatenated PCA, late fusion of separately trained models) should always be reported. Research gap Benchmarks with real ground truth for multi-omics (spike-in designs, simulations calibrated to real data, perturbation experiments). Integration that respects causal direction and time, rather than symmetric correlation. Uncertainty quantification for latent factors and integrated predictions. Scalable, privacy-preserving (federated) integration across cohorts that cannot share data. Interpretable deep integration: tying latent dimensions back to pathways and mechanisms. Mosaic integration with many missing layers, typical of clinical cohorts."}, {"id": "practice", "title": "Workflow & reproducibility", "text": "Practice Study design, workflows and reproducibility Most failed omics projects fail in design or data management, not in algorithms. This chapter covers the practical layer: design, batch effects, file formats, public data, workflow managers and reporting. Design before data Power : estimate from pilot or public data (RNA-seq: RNASeqPower, PROPER, ssizeRNA; scRNA-seq: scPower, hierarchicell; GWAS: genpwr). Biological replicates beat sequencing depth. Randomise and block : spread conditions across extraction days, library batches, flow cells and MS runs. Record everything in sample metadata. Controls : spike-ins (ERCC, SIRVs, sequins), pooled QC samples, reference materials (GIAB cell lines, Quartet multi-omics reference materials, NIST plasma), negative controls (blanks). Matched multi-omics : split the same aliquot across assays where possible; time from collection to freezing matters most for RNA and metabolites. Batch effects If batch is not confounded with the variable of interest, include it as a covariate in the model (preferred) or correct it: ComBat (empirical Bayes on continuous data), ComBat-seq (counts), limma removeBatchEffect (for visualisation only), RUVSeq/RUVg (using control genes), SVA (surrogate variables for unknown batches), and for single-cell, Harmony or scVI. Check before and after with PCA coloured by batch and by biology, kBET or LISI scores, and silhouette widths. Pitfall Correcting batch and then testing for group differences with the corrected data can exaggerate significance, especially when the design is unbalanced (Nygaard et al. 2016, Biostatistics). Model batch in the test instead of pre-correcting when possible. File formats you will meet Format Layer Contains FASTQ Sequencing Reads + per-base Phred qualities POD5 / BAM (unaligned) Long-read Nanopore raw signal / PacBio reads with kinetics or modification tags (MM/ML) SAM / BAM / CRAM Alignment Aligned reads, CIGAR strings, MAPQ, tags; CRAM is reference-compressed VCF / BCF / gVCF Variants Variant records, genotypes, annotations BED / narrowPeak / bigWig / bedGraph Intervals, signal Regions (0-based, half-open), peaks, coverage tracks GTF / GFF3 Annotation Genes, transcripts, exons (1-based, inclusive) .hic / .cool / .mcool / pairs 3D genome Contact matrices at multiple resolutions h5ad / h5Seurat / RDS / MuData / SpatialData (zarr) Single-cell, spatial Count matrices + cell and gene metadata + embeddings mzML / mzXML / raw Mass spectrometry Spectra; vendor raw files need conversion mzTab / mzIdentML Proteomics, metabolomics results Identifications and quantities BIOM / phyloseq / TreeSummarizedExperiment Microbiome Feature tables with taxonomy and trees Pitfall BED is 0-based half-open; GTF, VCF and SAM are 1-based. Off-by-one errors between them are among the most common bugs in genomics code. Public data you should know Raw and processed data GEO, SRA, ENA, DDBJ (sequencing) EGA, dbGaP (controlled human data) ArrayExpress / BioStudies PRIDE / ProteomeXchange, MassIVE (proteomics) MetaboLights, Metabolomics Workbench, GNPS (metabolomics) CELLxGENE Census, Human Cell Atlas, Single Cell Portal Reference cohorts TCGA / GDC, ICGC/PCAWG, CPTAC (cancer multi-omics) GTEx (tissue expression and QTLs) UK Biobank, All of Us, FinnGen, Biobank Japan gnomAD, 1000 Genomes, TOPMed, HPRC ENCODE, Roadmap, IHEC, 4DN (regulation) DepMap, LINCS/CMap (perturbations) Knowledge bases Ensembl, GENCODE, RefSeq, UCSC UniProt, AlphaFold DB, PDB GO, Reactome, KEGG, WikiPathways, MSigDB ClinVar, ClinGen, OMIM, HPO, Orphanet HMDB, ChEBI, LIPID MAPS GWAS Catalog, Open Targets, STRING, OmniPath Reproducible computation Workflow managers : Nextflow with the community-curated nf-core pipelines (rnaseq, sarek, atacseq, methylseq, scrnaseq, mag, ampliseq and more), Snakemake, WDL/Cromwell (Terra, GATK), CWL. Environments : conda/mamba (bioconda), Docker, Apptainer/Singularity for HPC; renv (R) and uv/poetry (Python) for analysis code. Version control : git for code; record reference genome build, annotation release, tool versions and parameters. DVC or data registries for large data. Notebooks and reports : Quarto, R Markdown, Jupyter. Keep exploratory notebooks separate from the final, scripted analysis. FAIR data : findable, accessible, interoperable, reusable. Use community metadata standards (MINSEQE, MIAPE, MIxS, Metabolomics Standards Initiative). Compute : WGS alignment and calling ≈ hours per genome on a server (minutes on GPU with Parabricks or DRAGEN); scRNA-seq with ~10 6 cells needs tens of GB of RAM or out-of-core tools; deep models need GPUs. Tools"}, {"id": "atlas", "title": "Tool atlas", "text": "Reference Tool atlas Every tool mentioned in the guide in one searchable table, with its aim, analysis stage and skill level. Filter by layer, stage or level, or type a task such as \"doublet\", \"peak\" or \"integration\". All layers All stages Primary Secondary Tertiary All levels Entry Intermediate Advanced"}, {"id": "benchmarks", "title": "Benchmark hub", "text": "Reference Benchmark hub What independent, neutral comparisons of tools have found. Benchmarks age quickly and can be biased by their choice of data and metrics, so read them for patterns and for what they measured, not only for the winner. How to read a benchmark Who ran it? Neutral benchmarks (by groups that did not develop any of the tools) are more trustworthy than the comparison table in a new tool's own paper, where the new tool almost always wins (the \"self-assessment trap\"). What is the ground truth? Simulations (controllable, but may not match reality), spike-ins and mixtures (realistic, limited), reference materials (GIAB, Quartet), orthogonal validation, or consistency only (weakest). Which metric? Precision, recall, F1, AUROC, AUPRC, FDR calibration, runtime, memory. A tool can win on one and lose on another. Check whether the metric matches your use. Does it match your data? Species, platform, depth, sample size and tissue all change rankings. A method that wins on cell lines may not win on tumour biopsies. Guidelines for running a good benchmark: Weber et al. 2019, Genome Biology . Continuous, community-maintained benchmarks (Open Problems in Single-Cell Analysis, OpenEBench, precisionFDA challenges, CAMI, CASP) are replacing one-off papers. All benchmarks All layers"}, {"id": "gaps", "title": "Research questions & gaps", "text": "Research Research questions and open gaps A map of what is unsolved, organised so you can find a thesis-sized problem. Each gap names why it is hard and what a contribution could look like. Strong PhD projects usually sit where a biological question, a data resource and a methodological weakness meet. Cross-cutting gaps 1 · Causality, not correlation Most multi-omics results are associations. Combining genetic anchors (QTLs, MR), perturbations (CRISPR, drugs) and time series to build causal multi-layer models is a central open problem. Contribution: a causal integration method validated against Perturb-seq or CRISPR screens. 2 · Ground truth for evaluation Many fields (spatial domains, GRNs, multi-omics integration, cell communication) lack ground truth, so methods cannot be compared fairly. Contribution: a benchmark dataset or calibrated simulator, plus a neutral comparison. 3 · Context specificity Variant and regulatory effects depend on cell type, state, stimulus and development. Current reference maps cover few contexts. Contribution: dynamic or single-cell QTL mapping; models that transfer effects across contexts. 4 · Diversity and equity Reference panels, GWAS, QTL resources and training data for predictors over-represent European ancestry, which limits accuracy elsewhere. Contribution: multi-ancestry fine-mapping, PRS or QTL methods; analysis of under-represented cohorts. 5 · Foundation models, honestly evaluated Large pre-trained models for DNA, RNA, proteins and cells are proliferating, but evaluations frequently show they do not beat simple baselines on key tasks. Contribution: rigorous evaluation, failure analysis, or task-specific models that demonstrably add value. 6 · Uncertainty and calibration Most pipelines output point estimates. Propagating uncertainty from raw signal to biological conclusion (quantification, imputation, latent factors) is rare. Contribution: Bayesian or conformal methods that give calibrated intervals. 7 · Interpretability Deep models predict well but do not explain. Linking latent dimensions to pathways and mechanisms remains hard. Contribution: biologically constrained architectures (pathway-informed, sparse) with validation. 8 · Scale, privacy and federation Biobanks with millions of participants and atlases with hundreds of millions of cells strain current algorithms; human data often cannot leave its institution. Contribution: scalable or federated algorithms, sketching, out-of-core methods. Gaps by layer Layer Open problem Why it is hard Possible project angle Genomics Interpreting non-coding and structural variants in rare disease Few labelled examples; effects are context-specific Integrate long-read SV calls with tissue RNA-seq outliers and regulatory maps to raise diagnostic yield Genomics Pangenome-aware variant calling and association Tools and coordinates assume a linear reference GWAS or QTL mapping on graph-genome genotypes; benchmarking in diverse samples Epigenomics Enhancer–gene linking and enhancer grammar Sparse CRISPR ground truth; redundancy between enhancers Sequence-to-function deep models (Enformer, Borzoi-style) evaluated on CRISPRi data in new cell types Epigenomics Haplotype-resolved methylation and allele-specific regulation Needed long reads only recently became affordable Joint phasing, methylation and expression from long-read multi-omics Transcriptomics Isoform-level function and quantification Short reads are ambiguous; long reads are lower depth Long-read differential isoform usage with uncertainty; isoform-to-proteoform validation with MS Single-cell Multi-sample, multi-condition statistics Nested design (cells within donors), compositional effects Mixed-model or pseudobulk frameworks for spatial and multi-modal data Spatial Segmentation, domains and cell–cell communication with validation Lack of ground truth; platform-specific artefacts Benchmarks combining imaging and sequencing spatial data from serial sections Perturbation Predicting responses to unseen perturbations and combinations Combinatorial space; models fail to beat linear baselines Causal or mechanistic priors for perturbation prediction; better evaluation metrics Proteomics Proteoforms and PTM crosstalk Bottom-up digestion loses combinatorial information Methods linking top-down and bottom-up data; PTM site-level integration with phospho-signalling models Proteomics Plasma proteomics platform harmonisation Olink vs SomaScan disagreement; epitope effects Cross-platform calibration using pQTLs and MS as anchors Metabolomics Annotating the dark metabolome Few reference spectra; isomers ML structure and class prediction from MS/MS; integrating genomic context (enzymes present) to constrain candidates Microbiome From composition to mechanism Compositional data, confounders, strain-level function Integrated metagenome–metabolome models with gnotobiotic or culture validation Integration Mosaic, temporal and causal integration Missing layers, different timescales, no ground truth Dynamic latent factor models; integration with genetic anchors (MR within MOFA-like frameworks) Clinical Translating multi-omics into decisions Small cohorts, overfitting, cost, regulatory evidence Minimal biomarker panels validated across cohorts; cost-effectiveness of adding layers How to choose a PhD problem in this field Start from a biological question that someone cares about (a disease, a developmental process, a drug response), then ask which layer limits the answer. Secure data early : public cohorts (TCGA, GTEx, UK Biobank, CELLxGENE), a collaborator's matched multi-omics, or your own. Many methods projects stall for lack of the right dataset. Find the methodological weakness using the benchmark hub: where do existing tools disagree, fail on certain data, or lack evaluation? Plan a validation : an orthogonal experiment, an independent cohort, a held-out perturbation, or a simulation calibrated to real data. Scope three papers : commonly (a) a benchmark or resource, (b) a method, (c) a biological application of the method. Release software as a documented package (Bioconductor, PyPI/scverse ecosystem) with tests; it multiplies the impact of the thesis. Ten concrete starting questions Do single-cell foundation model embeddings improve cell-type-specific eQTL mapping compared with pseudobulk on annotated clusters? Can long-read RNA-seq resolve which GWAS-colocalised sQTLs change protein-coding potential? Which share of GWAS loci without eQTL colocalisation are explained by caQTLs in disease-relevant cell types? How robust are multi-omics cancer subtypes (MOFA+, SNF, iCluster) across independent cohorts and platforms? Can cis-pQTL Mendelian randomisation and metabolite QTLs together identify enzyme targets with on-target metabolic biomarkers? Does integrating phosphoproteomics with transcriptomics (e.g. COSMOS) predict drug response better than either layer alone in cell-line panels? How much do spatial deconvolution results change with the choice of single-cell reference, and can this uncertainty be modelled? What fraction of untargeted metabolomic features in human plasma are microbial in origin, and can metagenomes predict them? Can calibrated variant effect predictors from protein language models reduce VUS rates in under-represented ancestries? How should a multi-omics study allocate a fixed budget between more samples and more layers?"}, {"id": "learn", "title": "Learning path & glossary", "text": "Reference Learning path and glossary A staged plan for becoming fluent in omics analysis, followed by the vocabulary you will meet in papers and pipeline documentation. Stages Stage Biology Computation Statistics Practice project 1 · Foundations Chapters 1–4 of this guide; a molecular biology textbook (Alberts, Molecular Biology of the Cell ) Linux shell, file formats, git; R basics (tidyverse) or Python (pandas) Distributions, hypothesis tests, regression, p-values vs effect sizes Download a GEO count matrix, make PCA and a volcano plot 2 · One layer well Gene regulation; the biology of your system Run nf-core/rnaseq; Bioconductor (SummarizedExperiment) or scverse (AnnData) GLMs, negative binomial, multiple testing, empirical Bayes Reanalyse a published RNA-seq study end to end and compare with the paper 3 · Single-cell and a second layer Cell types, development, chromatin Scanpy or Seurat; ATAC or proteomics pipelines; HPC job schedulers Dimensionality reduction, clustering, mixed models, compositional data Integrate two scRNA-seq datasets and test pseudobulk DE 4 · Integration and genetics Population genetics, QTLs PLINK, tensorQTL, MOFA+, mixOmics Latent factor models, Bayesian inference, causal inference (MR, colocalisation) Colocalise a GWAS with GTEx eQTLs; run MOFA+ on TCGA multi-omics 5 · Methods development Deep knowledge of one system PyTorch/JAX, package development, testing, benchmarking frameworks Probabilistic modelling, variational inference, ML evaluation Build and benchmark a method against neutral baselines Recommended resources Books Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell Strachan & Read, Human Molecular Genetics Holmes & Huber, Modern Statistics for Modern Biology (free online) James et al., An Introduction to Statistical Learning (free online) Durbin et al., Biological Sequence Analysis Online and free Single-cell Best Practices (sc-best-practices.org) Orchestrating Single-Cell Analysis with Bioconductor (OSCA) Bioconductor workflows and vignettes Galaxy Training Network nf-core documentation GATK Best Practices documentation Habits Reanalyse published data before generating your own Read the methods section of every tool you use Keep a lab notebook for analyses (date, versions, decisions) Follow benchmarks and preprints in your area (bioRxiv) Join communities: Biostars, Bioconductor support, scverse forum Glossary"}, {"id": "contact", "title": "Contact", "text": "About Contact Contact Questions, corrections, suggestions for tools or benchmarks to add, or ideas for collaboration are welcome. Name Kianoosh Naghibzadeh Email kianoosh.naghibzadeh@torontomu.ca Copy LinkedIn linkedin.com/in/kianooshnaghibzadeh City Toronto, Ontario, Canada Affiliations Toronto Metropolitan University TMU, Toronto The Hospital for Sick Children SickKids, Toronto About this guide The Omics Field Guide is a free, independent resource for students and researchers starting out in omics and bioinformatics. If you find an error or an outdated benchmark, please send an email with the chapter name and a reference, and it will be corrected in the next edition. If you use this guide in teaching or a publication, please cite it as: Naghibzadeh K. The Omics Field Guide. 2026. https://kianooshnaghibzadeh.github.io/omics-field-guide/"}];
